.TH OTPME 7 "2026-09-20" "OTPme 0.3.0a278" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme \- OTPme authentication and authorization system architecture
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B OTPme
is a system for authentication and authorization.
It supports multi-factor authentication, access control per service, and central management of users and their tokens.
This page describes how OTPme is built and its main concepts.
.PP
A main idea of OTPme is that each user can have
.B many authentication tokens
at the same time.
Different tokens can be used for different services.
So you decide for each service how a user logs in to it.
.PP
An example is WLAN.
Instead of one password on all devices of a user, the administrator gives each device its own password token (laptop, phone, tablet).
If a device is stolen, the administrator deletes the token of this device.
All other devices keep working.
There is no need to change the WLAN password on the other devices.
.PP
This also keeps the services apart.
A token only gives access to the services it is assigned to.
Someone who gets the WLAN password of a user can not use it for the user's mail, file shares or system login.
Each service has its own credentials.
.PP
For example, a user can log in to the workstation with a FIDO2 key and use the same FIDO2 token for file shares.
The same user can use WLAN with one password per device (RADIUS), and a separate password token for groupware (CalDAV, CardDAV, IMAP) on a smartphone that stores the password.
All of this is managed in one place, for one user.
.PP
Access is controlled with
.B clients
and
.BR "access groups" .
Each service that uses OTPme for authentication is a client object.
Each client has an access group.
Only tokens and roles in this access group can log in to the service.
.PP
A client can be a
.BR "RADIUS client" .
The RADIUS NAS identifier must be the same as the name of the OTPme client.
For example, a WLAN access point with the NAS ID "wlan\-office" needs an OTPme client named "wlan\-office".
Only tokens in the access group of this client are accepted for WLAN.
.PP
A client can also be an
.BR "LDAP client" ,
which uses
.BR otpme\-ldapd .
Here the client name is a DC part of the LDAP bind DN.
For example, the bind DN
.RS
.I uid=user1,ou=users,dc=mailserver,dc=example,dc=org
.RE
selects the OTPme client "mailserver".
So the mail server can only log in users with a token in the access group of the mail server.
.PP
OTPme has an
.BR "SSO web portal" .
Users log in there once and can then open the connected web applications without a new login.
The portal also has self service, so users can manage their own login methods:
.IP \(bu 2
account recovery, for example with a recovery mail address
.IP \(bu 2
device tokens: a separate password for each device, for example for WLAN or groupware
.IP \(bu 2
FIDO2 security keys and passkeys
.IP \(bu 2
TOTP tokens (authenticator apps)
.IP \(bu 2
tiqr tokens
.IP \(bu 2
a profile page with the user's photo and data.
The administrator decides which data users can change themselves (\fBsso_allow_profile_edit\fR).
.PP
A client can also be an
.B OIDC client
of the SSO portal, for example Nextcloud.
The web application sends its users to the OTPme SSO portal to log in (OpenID Connect).
Such a client is created with \fB\-\-enable\-oidc\fR, see
.BR otpme\-client (1).
Here too, only tokens and roles in the access group of the client can log in.
.SH ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
OTPme runs as a set of daemons.
\fBotpme\-controld\fR starts them.
Nodes run all daemons.
Hosts only run hostd and confd, and httpd or backupd if they are configured as SSO or backup server.
.SS Daemons
.TP
.B controld
Starts, stops and watches all other OTPme daemons.
.br
Runs on: nodes and hosts.
.TP
.B mgmtd
Management daemon.
It handles all changes to objects, for example adding, changing or deleting users, tokens and policies.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B authd
Authentication daemon.
It checks logins and credentials and manages login sessions.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B syncd
Sync server.
It sends objects to the hostd of hosts and of the nodes of other sites.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B hostd
Sync client.
It gets object changes from the syncd of the nodes.
On a node it syncs the objects of other sites; the objects of the own site are synced by clusterd.
.br
Runs on: nodes and hosts.
.TP
.B clusterd
Cluster daemon.
It manages the nodes of a site: quorum, the master node and failover.
It also syncs the objects of the own site between the nodes.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B joind
Join daemon.
It handles hosts and nodes that join the site.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B ldapd
LDAP server.
Other services can check logins and read directory data with LDAP.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
It is started by clusterd when the cluster has quorum and the node is in sync, and stopped when the quorum is lost.
.TP
.B httpd
Web server for the SSO portal (see DESCRIPTION) and for the site certificates (\fI/certs\fR).
.br
Runs on: nodes, and hosts with \fBSSO_SERVER\fR set in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
.B ssod
SSO daemon.
It does the work behind the SSO portal: logins, OIDC tokens and tiqr.
The web server talks to it over a local socket.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B fsd
File server for OTPme file shares, which clients mount with FUSE.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B scriptd
Runs scripts on the server, for example authentication scripts and push scripts.
.br
Runs on: nodes.
.TP
.B backupd
Runs backups.
.br
Runs on: nodes, and hosts with \fBBACKUP_SERVER\fR set in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
.B idled
Sends events to the \fBotpme\-agent\fR of logged in users, for example that a share can be mounted.
.br
Runs on: the master node only. clusterd starts it on the new master node when the master changes.
.TP
.B confd
Configuration daemon. It runs as root.
It gets signed configuration jobs from the nodes, checks them and runs them. See
.BR otpme\-deploy (1).
It also reports the OS and the installed packages of the host to the nodes. See
.BR otpme\-inventory (1).
.br
Runs on: nodes and hosts.
.SS External Integrations
.TP
.B FreeRADIUS
RADIUS logins, for example for WLAN and VPN.
OTPme comes with a FreeRADIUS module (rlm_python) that sends the RADIUS requests to authd.
FreeRADIUS can also use the command \fBotpme\-auth\fR instead of ntlm_auth.
Every application that supports RADIUS (for example with pam_radius_auth) can use OTPme for logins.
.TP
.B LDAP
\fBldapd\fR is an LDAP server.
Services like Nextcloud or a mail server can check logins and read users and groups with LDAP.
The client name in the bind DN selects the OTPme client, see DESCRIPTION.
.TP
.B OpenID Connect
The SSO portal is an OpenID Connect provider.
Web applications like Nextcloud use it to log in their users.
See \fB\-\-enable\-oidc\fR in
.BR otpme\-client (1).
.TP
.B tiqr
Login with the tiqr app on a smartphone.
Users add a tiqr token in the SSO portal and confirm logins in the app.
.TP
.B SSH
SSH keys can be stored in OTPme and assigned to hosts.
\fBotpme\-get\-authorized\-keys\fR \fIuser\fR prints the keys of a user for this host.
Use it as \fBAuthorizedKeysCommand\fR in sshd.
.TP
.B PAM Module
System login on hosts and nodes, see
.BR pam_otpme (8).
It works with password and OTP tokens and with hardware tokens like FIDO2 keys and YubiKeys (HMAC, PIV).
After the login the \fBotpme\-agent\fR is started.
It keeps the OTPme login session.
With it, users can manage OTPme objects and use the file shares of fsd without logging in again.
.TP
.B NSS (libnss\-cache)
OTPme users and groups are available as local Linux accounts through libnss\-cache.
hostd writes the cache files with nsscache.
.SS Recommended Token Type
The recommended token type is \fByubikey_piv\fR (a YubiKey with the PIV applet).
The sign key and the encrypt key of the user are stored on the YubiKey.
They can not be read from it, so they can not be copied from the computer either.
With them the user can safely:
.IP \(bu 2
use encrypted file shares
.IP \(bu 2
sign scripts, tokens and configuration jobs (see
.BR otpme\-deploy (1))
.IP \(bu 2
log in to the network with 802.1X port authentication.
\fBpam_otpme\fR creates a one-time password with the YubiKey at login and connects the LAN port with it, before the network is needed for the login. See \fBdo_dot1x\fR in
.BR pam_otpme (8).
.PP
The keys of the YubiKey only become the keys of the user if you deploy the token with \fB\-\-add\-user\-key\fR:
.RS
.B otpme\-token \-\-type yubikey_piv deploy \-\-add\-user\-key alice/piv
.RE
.PP
When the token is deployed, a backup of the keys is written.
With it a lost YubiKey can be replaced.
See \fBDeploy \- YubiKey PIV Applet\fR in
.BR otpme\-token (1).
.SH ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURE
OTPme has three levels: realms, sites and units.
.SS Realms
A
.B realm
is the top level, like a Windows domain.
It has its own users, groups, roles, policies, units and other objects.
Realms are independent from each other and can not be connected.
.PP
A realm has one or more sites.
Each user and each group belongs to one site, its home site, and is visible in all sites of the realm.
Other objects, for example units, roles and policies, only exist in their site.
.SS Sites
A
.B site
is a part of a realm with its own administration.
Usually there is one site per location.
The nodes of a site form a cluster, so each location has its own redundancy.
.PP
Each site has its own root user, who manages the users, groups and other objects of the site.
Each site also has the role
.BR SITE_ADMIN .
Add a token to this role to give its user admin rights for the site.
Other users can get rights for single objects with ACLs, also in other sites.
.PP
Token secrets are not synced to other sites by default.
When a user of site \fIberlin\fR logs in on a host of site \fImunich\fR, munich redirects the login to berlin.
If berlin trusts munich (site trust), the token secrets of berlin are synced to munich, and munich can check the login itself.
Add a trust with
.RS
.B otpme\-site add_trust \fIberlin\fB \fImunich\fR
.RE
or with \fB\-\-trust\fR when you add the site, see
.BR otpme\-site (1).
.PP
Users can be created in each site, or all in the master site.
Users in each site allow each location to manage its own users.
All users in the master site give one central administration.
.PP
A site trust is not needed for central users, but without it a slave site can not check logins itself.
If it can not reach the master site, nobody can log in there.
With a trust the slave site has the token secrets and logins keep working.
.PP
Nodes and hosts belong to one site, but their names must be unique in the whole realm.
New sites can only be added by the master site.
.SS Sub-Sites
A
.B sub-site
belongs to another site, its parent site.
Its name starts with the name of the parent site and a dot, for example \fBberlin.files\fR for the parent site \fBberlin\fR.
The CA of the sub-site is signed by the CA of the parent site.
A sub-site is added from its parent site, not from the master site.
.PP
Otherwise a sub-site is a normal site.
It is meant as a separate place for file shares.
The admins of the sub-site decide who can use its shares, not the admins of the parent site.
.PP
With SOTP signing (see
.BR otpme\-share (1)),
the admins of the parent site also can not create a login session for a user and use it to open the shares of the sub-site:
the login must be signed with the user's own sign key, for example on a YubiKey PIV.
.SS Units
.B Units
are like LDAP OUs.
They hold objects.
You can add ACLs and policies to a unit, for example:
.IP \(bu 2
ACLs that allow a token or role to add, change or delete objects in the unit
.IP \(bu 2
the policies \fBdefaultgroups\fR and \fBdefaultroles\fR, which add new users to groups and their default token to roles, see
.BR otpme\-policy (1)
.SH CORE OBJECT TYPES
.SS Users
A user is an identity that can log in.
Each user has a unique name and a unique UID (Unix user ID).
.PP
Every user has a default token, named \fIlogin\fR.
It is used to log in to the realm.
More tokens can be added for single services, for example one password token for the WLAN of the notebook and one for the smartphone.
.PP
Each user has a default group.
The group is not added to the user; the user is added to the group as a "default group user".
So an admin of site \fIberlin\fR can not give a user of berlin a group of site \fImunich\fR.
.PP
Users can also have policies and ACLs, for example an ACL that lets another user or token change this user, or a policy that says in which unit new objects of the user are created.
.PP
A user can have extensions.
By default a user has the extensions \fIbase\fR and \fIposix\fR.
Extensions say which LDAP attributes a user has, for example the mail address (base) or \fBuidNumber\fR and the home directory (posix).
.SS Tokens
A token is what a user logs in with.
There are these token types:
.IP \(bu 2
.B password
\- a static password. Password policies apply to it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B totp
\- one-time password by time (RFC 6238), for example from an authenticator app.
.IP \(bu 2
.B hotp
\- one-time password by counter (RFC 4226).
.IP \(bu 2
.B motp
\- mobile OTP (mOTP): the app makes the one-time password from a PIN and a secret.
.IP \(bu 2
.B otp_push
\- the one-time password is sent to the user (push).
.IP \(bu 2
.B fido2
\- hardware security keys and platform authenticators.
.IP \(bu 2
.B passkey
\- a passkey: the key stays on the device, the user logs in without a password.
.IP \(bu 2
.B u2f
\- older security keys (Universal 2nd Factor).
.IP \(bu 2
.B yubikey_hmac
\- YubiKey with HMAC-SHA1 challenge-response.
.IP \(bu 2
.B yubikey_piv
\- YubiKey with the PIV applet. The sign key and the encrypt key of the user are on the token. This is the recommended token type, see \fBRecommended Token Type\fR above.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ssh
\- login with an SSH key.
.IP \(bu 2
.B tiqr
\- the user confirms the login in the tiqr app on a smartphone.
.IP \(bu 2
.B link
\- points to a token of another user, so that token can be used here too.
.IP \(bu 2
.B script_otp
\- a script checks the one-time password.
.IP \(bu 2
.B script_static
\- a script checks a static password.
.PP
Only \fByubikey_piv\fR keeps the keys of the user on hardware.
For every other token type the private keys of the user are stored in OTPme, protected by the user's key password.
So \fByubikey_piv\fR is the only token type that gives safe access to encrypted shares.
.PP
SOTP signing works for shares and access groups, see
.BR otpme\-share (1)
and
.BR otpme\-accessgroup (1).
A stored key can be used for it, but then everything depends on the key password of the user:
an admin who controls the nodes gets the encrypted key, and a weak key password can be broken.
With \fByubikey_piv\fR there is no key to take.
.PP
For mgmtd, SOTP signing only works with \fByubikey_piv\fR.
A stored key comes from mgmtd itself, and mgmtd would already ask for a signed SOTP before it hands the key out.
.PP
With \fBpassword\fR it is also the only token type that can do 802.1X port authentication.
.PP
In OTPme, tokens are added to groups and roles, not users.
Usually this is the default token (\fIlogin\fR) of a user.
.PP
Tokens can also be used offline, so a user can still log in when the OTPme server can not be reached.
.SS Groups
A group of OTPme is also a Linux group on hosts and nodes, so you can use it for file permissions.
The group reaches a host through the sync: hostd writes the users and groups of the host into the NSS cache (see \fBNSS\fR above).
For this the group needs the \fIposix\fR extension, which new groups have by default.
.PP
To put a user into a group, add the default token of the user to the group.
You can also add the token to a role and the role to the group.
.PP
There is a group \fIrealmusers\fR with all users of the realm.
Each site also has a group with the name of the site, for example \fIberlin\fR, with all users of that site.
.PP
Groups can also be used as sync groups.
They then say which users are synced to a host.
.SS Dynamic Groups
A dynamic group is a local group on the host, not an OTPme group.
OTPme does not create it.
It has to exist on the host already, for example \fIdialout\fR, \fIvideo\fR or \fIwheel\fR.
At login pam_otpme puts the user into it for that session.
.PP
Use it for group memberships that only make sense on one host.
An OTPme group reaches every host the user is synced to.
A dynamic group stays where you set it.
.PP
You can set dynamic groups on a token, a role, a host and a node.
At login pam_otpme takes the groups of the host and the groups of the login token.
The groups of the roles of that token come with it.
A host gives only its own groups.
Its roles add nothing, because the groups of a host go to every login on it, also to a user who is in none of those roles.
.PP
A name that is already an OTPme group is refused.
Use that OTPme group itself instead.
.PP
A group that does not exist on the host is skipped.
pam_otpme writes a log message for it.
.PP
The host can limit which dynamic groups it takes.
In \fIotpme.conf\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBALLOW_DYNAMIC_GROUPS = False\fR turns dynamic groups off on this host.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBALLOW_DYNAMIC_GROUPS = wheel, dialout\fR allows only these two.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDENY_DYNAMIC_GROUPS = wheel\fR blocks these. It is checked first.
.PP
Without these two parameters every dynamic group is used.
.PP
The commands are \fBadd_dynamic_group\fR, \fBremove_dynamic_group\fR and \fBlist_dynamic_groups\fR.
They exist for tokens, roles, hosts and nodes.
.SS Roles
A role is a group of tokens.
Almost everywhere you can use a token, you can use a role instead.
Roles can also be added to other roles, so you can build them on top of each other.
.PP
There are two roles from the start:
.B REALM_USER
and
.BR SITE_ADMIN .
The default token of every user is added to REALM_USER.
With it the user can log in to the realm and is a member of the group \fIrealmusers\fR.
A token in SITE_ADMIN has admin rights for its site.
.PP
An example for roles:
.IP \(bu 2
the role \fIgroupware\fR gives access to the groupware (CalDAV, CardDAV, IMAP)
.IP \(bu 2
the role \fIemployee\fR is added to the role \fIgroupware\fR, so every employee has groupware
.IP \(bu 2
the role \fImarketing\fR is added to the role \fIemployee\fR and to the group \fImarketing\fR
.IP \(bu 2
the default token of a marketing user is added to the role \fImarketing\fR.
The user now has groupware access and is in the group \fImarketing\fR.
.PP
If all employees should get a new service later, for example a wiki, you only add the role \fIemployee\fR to the access group of the wiki.
.SS Access Groups
Access groups say who may use a service, and how its sessions work.
Every client gets an access group: a RADIUS client, an LDAP client or an OIDC client of the SSO portal.
For example the access point \fIap01\fR gets the access group \fIwlan\fR.
Then you add tokens or roles to the access group.
When a WLAN login arrives, the access group says who is let in.
.PP
An access group has more settings, for example how many failed logins lock a user out (\fBmax_fail\fR) and after how long the counter is reset (\fBmax_fail_reset\fR), see
.BR otpme\-accessgroup (1).
.PP
An access group can create a session for its logins.
This helps with services that ask again and again with the same password or OTP, for example an IMAP server.
Note that an OTP is then no longer used only once: it works as long as the session lives.
Per access group you can set how long a session lives and how many sessions a user may have at the same time.
.PP
An access group can have child access groups.
Tokens and roles of the parent group also have access to the children.
When a site is added, the access group \fIREALM\fR gets the children \fIMGMT\fR and \fILDAP\fR.
So a token that may log in to the realm can also use the management interface and LDAP.
.PP
The access group \fISSO\fR is different.
A token in it may log in to the SSO portal, but that alone gives no access to the connected applications.
For an application the token must also be in the access group of that application, for example \fInextcloud\fR.
.SS File Shares
A file share needs shared storage that is mounted on all nodes, for example under \fI/otpme\-mounts/\fR.
This mount point is the root directory of the share (\fBroot_dir\fR).
All nodes must see the same storage.
We recommend CephFS or NFS for it.
.PP
Add tokens or roles to a share to give them access to it.
Clients mount a share with FUSE, using
.BR otpme\-mount .
.PP
By default all nodes serve a share.
You can assign single nodes or a node pool to a share; then only those nodes serve it.
The client mounts the share from one of these nodes, chosen by chance, which spreads the load.
.PP
On the client the share is mounted below the mount root (\fBMOUNT_ROOT_DIR\fR, default \fI/otpme\fR):
.RS
.I /otpme/<user>/<realm>/<site>/<share>
.RE
.PP
A share has an ID, and that is what
.B otpme\-mount
takes:
.RS
.I <realm>/<site>/<share>
.RE
The realm is part of it because two realms may have a site of the same name.
You can leave it out and write \fI<site>/<share>\fR; then it is a share of your own realm.
.PP
A
.B home share
is a special share which gives every user an own directory.
A normal share gives all users the same directory.
In a home share, each user only gets the own directory and sees nothing else.
.PP
An example: the home share \fIhome\fR has the root directory \fI/otpme\-mounts/home\fR.
When user \fIalice\fR mounts it, OTPme creates the directory
.RS
.I /otpme\-mounts/home/alice
.RE
and hands out this directory as the root of the share.
On the computer of alice it is mounted as
.RS
.I /otpme/alice/example.org/berlin/home
.RE
The directory belongs to alice, and only alice can read it.
Its name is the user name (\fIalice\fR) or the UUID of the user; you choose that when you create the share.
.PP
A root mount token gets \fI/otpme\-mounts/home\fR itself as the root of the share, so it sees the directories of all users.
An encrypted home share encrypts each directory with an own key, and for that reason it has no root mount token.
See
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.PP
A share can be
.BR encrypted .
When it is created, you are asked for a master password, and the AES key of the share is made from it.
The data is encrypted on the client, so every token that may use the share needs its own copy of the AES key.
This copy is encrypted with the encrypt key of the user.
With \fByubikey_piv\fR this key is on the token, which is why this token type is the safe way to use encrypted shares.
Because each token needs its own key copy, only tokens can be added to an encrypted share, not roles.
.PP
A directory that is already an encrypted share can be taken over, for example after a move to another site, or when the share object is gone and the data is still there.
Create the share with \fB\-\-no\-key\-gen\fR, so OTPme does not make a new key: the data can only be read with the key it was written with.
The settings file in the directory stays as it is, so the key is still made the same way from the same master password.
Then mount the share once from the login session of root, with a token that may use the master password of it:
.RS
.B otpme\-mount \-\-master\-password\-mount \-\-add\-share\-key \fIshare mountpoint\fR
.RE
OTPme asks for the master password, makes the key from it and stores it for your token.
After that the share mounts the normal way.
See
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.PP
A share can also use SOTP signing.
The login to the share must then be signed with the sign key of the user, see
.BR otpme\-share (1)
and \fBTokens\fR above.
.SS Node Pools
A node pool is a group of nodes.
Assign a pool to a share, and only the nodes of this pool serve the share.
.SS Policies
A policy is a rule that OTPme applies to objects.
You can add a policy to units, users, tokens, access groups and other objects.
A policy on a unit applies to the objects in that unit.
.TP
.B authonaction
Ask for a new authentication before an action, for example before a user is deleted or an ACL is added.
You can set how long an authentication stays valid, and which tokens and roles are exempt.
.TP
.B autodisable
Disable an object after some time, for example \fB+1h\fR, \fB+1D\fR, \fB+1W\fR, \fB+1M\fR or \fB+1Y\fR.
The time can also count from the last use of the object instead of the time it was assigned.
.TP
.B defaultgroups
Add new users to the given groups, and set the default group of a user.
.TP
.B defaultpolicies
Add the given policies to new objects of a type.
.TP
.B defaultroles
Add the default token of new users to the given roles.
.TP
.B defaultunits
Say in which unit new objects of a type are created.
.TP
.B forcetoken
Allow only some token types for a login, for example only \fBfido2\fR and \fByubikey_piv\fR.
You can also allow only some kinds of credentials, for example \fBotp\fR, \fBssh_key\fR, \fBsmartcard\fR or \fBtiqr\fR.
Add this policy to an access group, a client, a host, a node or a role.
.TP
.B idrange
Set the ranges for the LDAP attributes \fBuidNumber\fR and \fBgidNumber\fR that OTPme gives to new users and groups.
The numbers can be given in order or at random.
.br
Add this policy to a unit or to a site.
A new object asks its unit first and its site second, so a site-wide range is the fallback for every unit that names none.
.TP
.B logintimes
Allow logins only at certain times, written like a cron entry, for example only on work days or only during work hours.
.TP
.B objecttemplates
Create new users or hosts from a template, so they start with the settings of that template.
.TP
.B password
Set what a password or PIN must look like: how long it must be, which characters it needs, and whether it may be a word from a dictionary.
.TP
.B tokenacls
Give new tokens their ACLs, which say what the user and the creator of a token may do with it.
.SH ACCESS CONTROL
OTPme has two kinds of access control:
.IP \(bu 2
Access groups say who may log in to a service, see \fBAccess Groups\fR above.
.IP \(bu 2
ACLs say who may see and change objects.
.SS ACLs (Access Control Lists)
An ACL says what a token or a role may do with an object.
Add one with \fBadd_acl\fR, for example:
.RS
.B otpme\-token add_acl \fIalice/pass\fB role \fIhelpdesk\fB "edit:password"\fR
.RE
Every token in the role \fIhelpdesk\fR may now change the password of the token \fIalice/pass\fR.
Use \fBlist_acls\fR to see the ACLs of an object and \fBdel_acl\fR to remove one.
.PP
An ACL has a permission and, after a colon, what it applies to.
\fBenable:object\fR for example only allows to enable the object itself, while \fBenable\fR alone allows every enable permission of it (\fBenable:object\fR, \fBenable:mschap\fR and so on).
A permission that names one thing is written in the singular (\fBadd:token\fR), one that shows a list in the plural (\fBview:tokens\fR).
.PP
There are three kinds of ACLs.
They differ by the \fB+\fR in front of them:
.TP
.B acl
A normal ACL, for example \fBedit:password\fR.
It applies to the object it is set on, and only to that object.
.TP
.B +acl
A default ACL, for example \fB+user:edit\fR on a unit.
It is not used for the unit itself.
It is given to the objects in the unit, as a normal ACL (without the \fB+\fR).
So the objects get the ACL, but do not pass it on.
.TP
.B ++acl
A recursive default ACL, for example \fB++user:edit\fR.
Like a default ACL, but a unit in the unit keeps it as \fB++acl\fR.
So it goes down through all units below, and every object there gets it.
.PP
An example: with
.RS
.B otpme\-unit add_acl \fIhosts\fB role \fIhelpdesk\fB "++host:edit"\fR
.RE
every host in the unit \fIhosts\fR and in all units below it can be changed by the role \fIhelpdesk\fR, also the hosts you add later.
.PP
A new object gets the default ACLs of its unit when it is created.
Objects that are already there do not, unless you say so: \fB\-a\fR applies the default ACLs to the objects of the unit, \fB\-r\fR also to the objects in the units below, see
.BR otpme\-unit (1).
.PP
An object can refuse inherited ACLs with \fBdisable_acl_inheritance\fR.
\fBenable_acl_inheritance\fR turns it on again.
.PP
Which ACLs a kind of object knows is in
.BR otpme\-acls (7).
\fBlist_acls\fR shows the ACLs an object has, and the shell completion offers the ones it takes after \fBadd_acl\fR.
.SH OFFLINE AUTHENTICATION
When a host can not reach the OTPme server, it can check logins against data it saved at the last login.
This has to be switched on for each token (\fBenable_offline\fR).
.PP
At an online login the host saves the token and the session, encrypted.
The key for it comes from the password, the PIN or the answer of the smartcard of the user (Argon2i).
.PP
While the server can not be reached, the host checks logins against this saved data.
It notes which OTPs were used, so an OTP can not be used a second time.
.PP
Two timeouts say how long the saved data may be used:
\fBoffline_expiry\fR counts from the last online login, \fBoffline_unused_expiry\fR from the last use.
See
.BR otpme\-token (1).
.PP
When the server can be reached again, hostd sends the used OTPs and the token counters back to it.
.SH CONFIGURATION MANAGEMENT
OTPme can run configuration jobs on hosts, for example install packages, add apt repositories, write files or restart services.
Jobs are created with
.BR otpme\-deploy (1).
.PP
A job is signed with the sign key of the user who creates it.
The \fBconfd\fR daemon on each host gets its jobs from the nodes, checks the signature and runs the job.
The result goes back to the nodes.
.PP
Two checks must pass before a job runs:
.IP \(bu 2
The nodes check that the user may create jobs for the host (config parameter \fBallow_confd_jobs\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
The host checks that the job is signed by a signer it trusts for this job type.
The job types are also signer types: \fBsw_mgmt\fR, \fBrepo_mgmt\fR, \fBsigner_mgmt\fR, \fBservice_mgmt\fR, \fBfile_mgmt\fR and \fBhost_mgmt\fR.
Add them on the host with \fBotpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type\fR \fIjob_type\fR.
.PP
So the nodes decide who may ask, and each host decides what may be done to it.
A node that is taken over can not run jobs on a host without a valid signature.
.PP
Jobs and results are only synced between the nodes of the site. Other sites do not get them.
Package jobs work on Debian and Ubuntu hosts.
.PP
confd does not run a job itself.
It starts a service of its own for each job (\fBotpme\-confd\-job\fR).
So a long job does not block confd, and a job that restarts OTPme keeps running and can report its result afterwards.
.PP
confd also reports which system the host runs and which packages are installed.
You can see and search this with
.BR otpme\-inventory (1).
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
Config parameters are set on an object with the \fBconfig\fR command.
\fBshow_config\fR shows them.
Set a parameter with:
.RS
.B otpme\-site config \fIberlin\fB \fIparameter\fB \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
.RE
Remove it again with \fB\-d\fR:
.RS
.B otpme\-site config \-d \fIberlin\fB \fIparameter\fR
.RE
.PP
A parameter is looked up at the object itself, then at its unit, then at its site.
The first value found wins, so a value on a site or unit is the default for the objects below it.
A site can also enforce a parameter for all of its objects, see \fBforce_site_config_parameters\fR below.
Above the site nothing is asked: a realm holds no config parameters.
The line \fIObject types\fR of each parameter says on which objects it can be set.
.PP
When a site is created, OTPme writes the default of every parameter into that site.
The site keeps these values.
Two things follow from that.
.PP
A parameter that OTPme got after the site was created is not in \fBshow_config\fR of that site, and it has no value there.
What an unset parameter means is up to the code that reads it, so better set it on the site.
.PP
A new default in OTPme does not reach a site that already has the old value.
Set the new value on the site yourself.
.PP
Some parameters take a time.
The unit is one letter behind the number:
.RS
.TP
.B s
seconds
.TP
.B m
minutes
.TP
.B h
hours
.TP
.B D
days
.TP
.B W
weeks
.TP
.B M
months
.TP
.B Y
years
.RE
.IP
The letters are case sensitive.
\fBm\fR is minutes and \fBM\fR is months, and a day is \fBD\fR, never \fBd\fR.
A number without a unit means seconds.
.PP
The same units are used outside of the config parameters, for example by \fB\-\-auto\-disable\fR.
A relative time is written with a leading \fB+\fR there, e.g. \fB+30D\fR.
.PP
A parameter of type \fBtime\fR is stored as seconds and shown in these units, in the largest unit that fits.
So 86400 is shown as \fB1D\fR and 7776000 as \fB3M\fR.
A parameter of type \fBint\fR that takes a time is shown as the number of seconds.
.SS General
.TP
.B confirmation_policy \fR(str, default: paranoid)
When OTPme asks before a change.
\fBparanoid\fR asks for every change, and for deleting you have to type the name of the object.
\fBnormal\fR only asks when you delete something, also with the name.
\fBforce\fR never asks.
The option \fB\-f\fR skips the questions with every policy.
.br
Object types: site, unit, token
.TP
.B auto_sign \fR(bool, default: false)
Offer to sign the object after each change.
.br
On an object a token can be assigned to \(em a role, a group, an access group, a client, a host or a node \(em it says to sign the token for that assignment, which is what
.B add_token \-\-sign
does by hand.
A host can be told to let an SSH key in only with such a signature, see
.BR otpme\-role (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token, script, role, group, accessgroup, client, host, node
.TP
.B auto_revoke \fR(bool, default: true)
Revoke the signatures of an object when it is changed.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token, script
.TP
.B force_site_config_parameters \fR(list)
Config parameters that the site enforces for all of its objects.
For a parameter in this list the value of the site wins.
The values on the objects below it (units, users, tokens and so on) are not used at all.
.br
If the site has no value for such a parameter, the values below are ignored as well, and the default of the parameter applies.
.br
Only parameters that are valid for a site can be enforced, and this parameter can not enforce itself.
Add more values with \fB\-a\fR, remove single ones with \fB\-d\fR.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B changelog \fR(bool, default: true)
Write the commands that change this object into its changelog.
Looked up at the object, then at its unit, then at its site.
Switch it off for objects that change very often.
A site that wants its own setting everywhere adds \fBchangelog\fR to
.BR force_site_config_parameters .
.br
Object types: all tree objects (site, unit, user, token, ...), but not the realm
.TP
.B allow_changelog_clearing \fR(bool, default: true)
Allow \fBclear_changelog\fR on this object.
Switch it off to keep the changelog of an object complete.
.br
Object types: all tree objects (site, unit, user, token, ...), but not the realm
.TP
.B ldap_verify_acls \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether \fBotpme\-ldapd\fR checks the ACLs of the objects it hands out.
With the check, an attribute only goes to a token that holds the ACL \fBview:attribute:\fIattr\fR on the object, or that is in \fBldif_whitelist_attributes\fR.
Switch it off to let a token read every attribute of every object it can find, for example for a service account that syncs the directory.
.br
The value is looked up at the token that asks, then at its user, its unit and its site.
So you can switch it off for one token without opening the whole site.
For admin tokens the ACLs are never checked.
.br
Can only be changed by an admin.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B ldap_on_request_attributes \fR(list)
LDIF attributes that \fBotpme\-ldapd\fR only returns when the search asks for them by name.
A search that asks for all attributes gets everything but these.
Unset means nothing is held back.
.br
This keeps big attributes out of searches that do not need them.
A client that asks for all attributes in every search (SOGo does that in every address book search) would otherwise pull the photo of every hit over the network.
Set \fBjpegPhoto\fR here to stop that.
A search with "*" asks for all user attributes.
\fBRFC 4511\fR (4.5.1.8) does not promise that a server returns all of them: it tells client implementors that attributes can be missing "due to access controls or other restrictions".
So a client has to cope with it.
.br
The value is looked up at the token that asks, then at its user, its unit and its site.
A client that really needs these attributes in every search can get an empty list on its token.
Upper and lower case in attribute names does not matter.
Add more values with \fB\-a\fR, remove single ones with \fB\-d\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B ldif_whitelist_attributes \fR(list, default: dn, objectClass, uid, cn, displayName, entryUUID, l, mail, mailLocalAddress)
LDIF attributes that are handed out without the ACL \fBview:attribute:\fIattr\fR.
Every other attribute only goes to a token that holds this ACL on the object.
.br
A value on the token that asks wins, so one token can see more or less than the rest of the site.
Without such a value the list comes from the site that answers the LDAP request (see \fBotpme\-ldapd\fR), not from the site of the object, and it applies to all objects of that site.
.br
The default is what an LDAP client needs to find an object at all.
Make it shorter to hide the mail address, for example, or longer to show more attributes to every user that is logged in.
Add more values with \fB\-a\fR, remove single ones with \fB\-d\fR.
.br
Can only be changed by an admin.
.br
Object types: site, token
.TP
.B allow_non_admin_trash_empty \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether tokens that are no admin may run \fBotpme\-trash empty\fR.
If they may, they only empty the trash entries of their own token.
If they may not, only admins can empty the trash.
The value is looked up at the token that asks, then at its user, its unit and its site.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS Key Backup
.TP
.B private_key_backup_key \fR(str)
The public RSA key (base64) that the private keys of users are encrypted with for the backup.
When a YubiKey PIV token is deployed below this object, the backup of its keys is encrypted with this key (\fB\-\-backup\fR and \fB\-\-restore\-from\-server\fR, see
.BR otpme\-token (1)).
Whoever has the private part of this key can decrypt the backups of all these tokens.
.br
If the parameter has no value, OTPme creates a key pair when you set it.
This happens on the computer where you run the command: only the public key goes to OTPme, the private key is written to a file you choose (\fI/dev/shm/<name>.pem\fR by default).
Keep this file in a safe place outside of OTPme, for example on an encrypted medium in a safe.
Without it the key backups of the tokens can not be restored.
.br
Can only be changed by an admin.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B private_key_backup_key_len \fR(int, default: 4096)
How long the key from \fBprivate_key_backup_key\fR is, in bits.
Allowed: 2048, 3072 and 4096.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.SS Password Hashing
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_type \fR(str, default: Argon2_i)
Which hash new password tokens use.
Possible values: \fBArgon2_i\fR, \fBArgon2_d\fR, \fBArgon2_id\fR, \fBPBKDF2\fR and \fBHKDF\fR.
The settings of each hash are further down, see \fBHash Parameters\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B session_hash_type \fR(str, default: Argon2_i)
Which hash is used for session passwords.
Same values as \fBdefault_pw_hash_type\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS User Scripts
Every user has scripts for a few tasks.
These parameters say which script a new user gets.
The scripts themselves are managed with
.BR otpme\-script (1).
.PP
Before a script runs, OTPme checks its signatures against the signers of that script type on this host or node (\fBotpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type\fR \fIscript_type\fR).
This only happens when there is such a signer.
Without one the script runs unchecked.
.PP
\fBHow a script gets its values\fR
.PP
OTPme has a set of values for each script, for example the user and the token of a login.
The script options say how the script gets them.
Set the options together with the script, for example with \fBotpme\-user auth_script\fR \fIuser\fR \fIscript\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIoptions\fR, see
.BR otpme\-user (1).
There are three forms:
.RS
.TP
.BI [ NAME ]
The value goes into the environment of the script, under this name.
.TP
.BI % name
The value becomes a command line argument (\fB$1\fR, \fB$2\fR, ... in the order of the options).
.TP
.I anything else
goes to the command line as it is.
.RE
.PP
Without any options the script gets every value in its environment, under the name of the value in small letters (\fBauth_user\fR and so on).
A value that is not set is left out.
.PP
An example:
.RS
.B otpme\-user auth_script \fIalice\fB \fIscripts/auth_script.sh\fB \-\- [AUTH_USER] %auth_client \-v\fR
.RE
The script then has \fBAUTH_USER\fR in its environment, gets the client as \fB$1\fR and \fB\-v\fR as \fB$2\fR.
A value that is not set becomes an empty argument here.
.PP
\fBThe values of an auth script\fR
.PP
\fBauth_type\fR, \fBauth_user\fR, \fBauth_token\fR, \fBauth_group\fR, \fBauth_client\fR, \fBauth_client_ip\fR, \fBauth_pass\fR, \fBauth_otp\fR, \fBauth_challenge\fR, \fBauth_response\fR and \fBauth_nt_key\fR.
Which of them have a value depends on the kind of login: \fBauth_pass\fR with a password, \fBauth_otp\fR with a one-time password, and the challenge and response values with MSCHAP and SSH.
.PP
The script allows the login with exit code 0 and denies it with every other code.
For an MSCHAP login it also has to write \fBNT_KEY: \fIkey\fR to stdout.
.TP
.B default_key_script \fR(str, default: scripts/key_script.sh)
Works with the private keys of the user: signing, decrypting and changing the key password.
With a \fByubikey_piv\fR token it talks to the YubiKey instead of a stored key.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_auth_script \fR(str, default: scripts/auth_script.sh)
Runs on the node during a login.
It gets the kind of login, the user, the access group, the token and the client, and can allow or deny the login.
Use it for checks of your own.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_agent_script \fR(str, default: scripts/agent_script.sh)
Starts the agent of the user at the login through PAM, for example ssh\-agent or gpg\-agent, with the right options.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_login_script \fR(str, default: scripts/login_script.sh)
Runs on the host at the login through PAM, so you can do things there.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B run_foreign_user_auth_scripts \fR(bool, default: false)
If enabled, a login of a user of another site may run the auth scripts of that user.
.br
The auth script of a user, and the auth script of their token, are scripts of the site of that user.
Running one means running code of that site here, on your node, at every login of that user.
.br
Off by default.
The login then goes on without these scripts.
The auth scripts of your own users always run.
.br
Object types: site
.SS User Management
.TP
.B user_photo_dimensions \fR(str, default: 300x450)
How big the photo of a user is, as \fIwidth\fBx\fIheight\fR in pixels.
Each side must be between 16 and 4096.
.br
A photo of another size is not taken as it is: OTPme asks whether to resize it, and refuses it if you say no.
\fB\-f\fR resizes without asking, and the profile page of the SSO portal asks in the browser.
.br
Resizing makes the photo big enough to cover the size and cuts off the rest around the middle.
So a photo with another ratio of width and height loses the same amount on both sides instead of looking squeezed.
The EXIF orientation of the photo is used.
Photos that are already stored stay as they are.
.br
The value is looked up at the user, then at the unit, then at the site.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B failed_pass_history \fR(int, default: 16)
How many wrong passwords OTPme remembers per user.
The same wrong password again is not counted as a new failure, so a client that repeats an old password does not lock the user out.
The locking itself is done by the access group, see \fBmax_fail\fR in
.BR otpme\-accessgroup (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B add_default_token \fR(bool, default: true)
Create a default token for every new user.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_token_name \fR(str, default: login)
The name of this default token.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_token_type \fR(str, default: hotp)
The type of this default token.
Every token type of OTPme can be used, see \fBTokens\fR above.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_sso_token_name \fR(str, default: sso\-<realm>\-<site>)
The name of the token that the SSO portal deploys, recovers and gives the role of the default token to.
It must be another name than \fBdefault_token_name\fR.
.br
The parameter belongs to the site, and OTPme reads it from the site of the portal the user is using, not from the site of the user.
So a user of one site who logs in at the portal of another site gets the name of that portal.
.br
Two sites with two names are what lets one hardware security key work for both.
Each site then has its own OTPme token, and each token its own WebAuthn credential for the RP ID of that portal.
The default name already contains realm and site, so two sites never get the same name by accident.
.br
This parameter does not create the token.
An admin adds it and puts it into the roles of that site.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B user_key_len \fR(int, default: 3072)
How long the RSA keys of a user are, in bits.
Allowed: 2048, 3072, 4096 and 8192.
With a \fByubikey_piv\fR token the keys are made on the token instead, see \fB\-\-key\-len\fR in
.BR otpme\-token (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B allow_default_token_rename \fR(bool, default: false)
If enabled, users are allowed to rename their default token.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B allow_default_token_deletion \fR(bool, default: false)
If enabled, users are allowed to delete their default token.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B allow_temp_passwords \fR(bool, default: false)
Allow a temporary password on a token (\fBotpme\-token temp_password\fR).
The token then also works with this password, for one hour by default (\fB\-\-duration\fR), and after that only with its own credentials again.
.br
This is for an admin or the IT support, to work as the user for a while, for example to set up a notebook for that user.
The SSO portal can do the same, see \fBadmin_access_role\fR.
See
.BR otpme\-token (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B allow_sso_account_recovery \fR(bool, default: false)
The main switch of the token recovery in the SSO portal, the link \fBCan't sign in?\fR on the login page (see \fBallow_sso_token_recovery\fR).
.br
With it off, asking for a recovery mail gets the same answer as asking for a user that does not exist, so nobody learns whether an account is there.
A recovery link that somebody still has is refused.
.br
This switch says whether recovery is offered at all, \fBallow_sso_token_recovery\fR says which token types it may hand out.
Whether the login page shows the link to it is \fBsso_show_recover_link\fR.
.br
The value is looked up at the user, then at the unit, then at the site, on the home site of the user.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B allow_sso_token_recovery \fR(list, default: empty)
The token types a user may get back through the SSO portal.
A user who lost the token asks for a mail, and the mail holds a link to deploy the token again.
.br
This always works on the SSO token of the user, the one named by \fBdefault_sso_token_name\fR.
If the type of that token is not in this list, recovery is refused.
The default is an empty list, so recovery is off.
.br
The mail address for it is kept on the user, in the LDAP attribute \fBotpmeRecoveryMail\fR.
The value is looked up at the user, then at the unit, then at the site, on the home site of the user.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_recovery_link_ttl \fR(int, default: 900)
How long a recovery link from the mail works, in seconds.
Time units are allowed, for example \fB15m\fR or \fB2h\fR.
It must be between one minute and 24 hours: a typo like \fB1\fR (one second) fails at once, and nobody opens a recovery window of days by accident.
.br
The value is looked up at the user, then at the unit, then at the site, on the home site of the user.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_recovery_mail_from \fR(str)
The sender of the recovery mail, used as envelope sender and as \fBFrom:\fR.
The home site of the user sends the mail, over the \fBsmtp_relay_*\fR parameters.
There is no default: without a sender no mail is sent, and recovery does not work.
.br
The text of the mail is the info text of the \fBSSO\fR access group of that site, one text per language (\fBotpme\-accessgroup info \-\-language\fR, see
.BR otpme\-accessgroup (1)).
A text written without a language is the note of the admin and is never sent.
.br
The first line of the text is the subject, then comes an empty line, then the body.
\fB{user_name}\fR, \fB{minutes}\fR and \fB{reset_url}\fR are filled in.
.br
A new site gets the English text that OTPme brings with it.
If the access group has no text for the language of the user, OTPme uses the English one, and if that is missing too, the text it brings with it, translated into the language of the user.
The language is the one of the user (\fBotpme\-user language\fR), or the one the browser asked for when the user has none.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B admin_access_role \fR(str)
The SSO portal has an admin access: a member of this role can set a temporary password on the token of another user there.
The admin or the IT support can then work as this user for a while, for example to set up a notebook (see \fBallow_temp_passwords\fR).
.br
The value is looked up at the token, then at the user, then at the unit, then at the site.
For a user of another site this only happens when the local site trusts that site, see \fBadmin_access_trusts\fR.
Without that trust only the value of the local site itself is used.
.br
Can only be changed by an admin.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B admin_access_trusts \fR(list)
The sites this site trusts for \fBadmin_access_role\fR.
When a user of one of these sites opens an admin access here, OTPme uses the role from the user of that site.
For every other site only the \fBadmin_access_role\fR of this site is used.
Users of the own site are always trusted.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B password_allowed_chars \fR(str, default: 0-9A-Za-z!@#$%&*()_+-={}[]:;<>.?/)
Which characters a password may have.
Ranges like \fB0\-9\fR are allowed.
The password policy checks this, see
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.SS Certificates
Every site has its own CA.
It issues the certificates of the nodes and hosts of the site, and the certificate of the site itself, which is used for TLS and for RADIUS.
The CA of a site is signed by the CA of the realm, the CA of a sub-site by the CA of its parent site.
.TP
.B cert_key_len \fR(int, default: 3072)
How long the RSA key of a new certificate is, in bits.
Allowed: 2048, 3072, 4096 and 8192.
.br
Object types: site, unit, ca
.TP
.B cert_sign_algo \fR(str, default: sha256)
The hash a certificate is signed with.
Allowed: \fBsha256\fR, \fBsha384\fR and \fBsha512\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, ca
.TP
.B crl_sign_algo \fR(str, default: sha256)
The hash a certificate revocation list (CRL) is signed with.
Same values as \fBcert_sign_algo\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, ca
.SS File Shares
.TP
.B default_share_add_script \fR(str, default: scripts/add_share.sh)
The script a new share gets as its add script.
It runs once, when the share is created, and sets up the directories of the share.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B default_share_mount_script \fR(str, default: scripts/mount_share.sh)
The script a new share gets as its mount script.
It runs every time a user mounts the share.
Use it to mount the storage behind the share at the first access, for example an NFS or CephFS backend.
.br
Both scripts are run by scriptd as \fBROOT_SCRIPT_USER\fR and \fBROOT_SCRIPT_GROUP\fR (\fBnobody\fR and \fBnogroup\fR by default, see
.BR otpme.conf (5)),
never as root and never as the OTPme user.
So a script that has to mount something or write outside its own directories needs \fBsudo\fR for these commands.
See
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B share_root \fR(str, default: /otpme\-mounts/)
The directory that holds the shares of this site.
A new share gets \fIshare_root\fB/\fIshare name\fR as its own directory, so with the default a share named \fIprojects\fR gets \fI/otpme\-mounts/projects\fR.
.br
It is also the boundary for the directory of a share: a share can only have a directory below \fBshare_root\fR.
That is why only an admin can change this parameter, and not everybody who may edit a share.
.br
The directory must exist when you set the parameter.
\fBSHARE_ROOT\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5)
wins over it on a single host.
.br
Can only be changed by an admin.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.SS Hosts
.TP
.B allow_confd_jobs \fR(list)
Tokens and roles that may create configuration jobs for a host (see
.BR otpme\-deploy (1)).
Give them as a comma separated list of OIDs, for example \fBrole|example.org/berlin/deploy\-admins\fR or \fBtoken|example.org/berlin/alice/login\fR. A token counts if it is in one of the roles, also through nested roles.
The OIDs are stored as UUIDs, so renaming a role or token keeps the permission.
.br
The value is looked up for each target host; the most specific match wins (host or node overrides unit overrides site). So a role can be allowed for the hosts of one unit only.
Unset (default) means nobody may create jobs.
.br
This only decides who may create a job. The host still checks the job signature against its own signers.
.br
Object types: site, unit, host, node
.TP
.B conf_job_max_age \fR(time, default: 3M, i.e. 90 days)
How long configuration jobs and their results are kept on the nodes.
It takes time units, e.g. \fB180D\fR.
The lowest value is one day.
Jobs that worked are kept too, so you can see which jobs were already sent.
A job that failed on a host is kept until it is acknowledged with \fBotpme\-deploy ack\fR, unless it was taken back with \fBotpme\-deploy cancel\fR \(em that answers it as well.
The \fBconfd\fR daemon on the master node removes old jobs once an hour.
.br
A remote access job goes by
.B remote_access_job_max_age
instead.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B remote_access_job_max_age \fR(time, default: 1h)
How long a remote access job is kept after it expired.
It takes time units, e.g. \fB30m\fR, and \fB0\fR means as soon as it expires.
.br
Its own value, because such a job is a different kind of record: \fBotpme\-shell\fR makes one for every shell, it is good for five minutes, and it says nothing afterwards \(em whoever asked for the shell stood there while it happened.
Kept as long as every other job they would pile up for three months.
.br
Counted from the expiry and not from the creation, because that is when the job stopped being of use to anybody.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B hosts_accessgroup \fR(str)
The access group that every new host is added to.
The value is the name of an access group that exists.
.br
This is for MAC Authentication Bypass (MAB): a device that can not log in by itself, for example a printer, is let into the network by its MAC address.
The switch asks OTPme with that address, and the access group of the host says whether it is let in.
.br
You need the \fBadd:host\fR ACL on that access group to set this parameter, because the hosts are added to it later without another check.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B hosts_role \fR(str)
The role that every new host is added to.
The value is the name of a role that exists, and it must be a role of the same site.
You can also write it as \fIsite\fB/\fIrole\fR, which is how a role is shown.
.br
You need the \fBadd:host\fR ACL on that role to set this parameter, because the hosts are added to it later without another check.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.SS Devices
A device is network equipment that can not log in by itself, for example an IP phone or a printer.
It only has a MAC address and is let into the network with MAC Authentication Bypass (MAB), see
.BR otpme\-device (1).
A host is more than that: it has joined the realm and runs hostd.
A host can have a MAC address too, so it can also be used for MAB.
.TP
.B devices_accessgroup \fR(str)
The access group that every new device is added to.
The value is the name of an access group that exists.
The switch asks OTPme with the MAC address of the device, and this access group says whether it is let in.
.br
You need the \fBadd:device\fR ACL on that access group to set this parameter, because the devices are added to it later without another check.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B devices_role \fR(str)
The role that every new device is added to.
The value is the name of a role that exists, and it must be a role of the same site.
You can also write it as \fIsite\fB/\fIrole\fR, which is how a role is shown.
.br
You need the \fBadd:device\fR ACL on that role to set this parameter, because the devices are added to it later without another check.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.SS VLAN
.TP
.B vlans \fR(list)
The VLANs that a port gets after an 802.1X or MAB login.
Each value is the name of a VLAN object, see
.BR otpme\-vlan (1).
.br
This is the second of the two ways to assign a VLAN, and the one that is asked last.
A VLAN of the site that answers the RADIUS request can name the object itself, or a role the object is in, and such a membership wins over this parameter every time.
Only where no VLAN of that site names the object is this parameter looked at, see
.BR otpme\-vlan (1).
.br
The value is looked up at the token, then at the user, then at the host or device, then at the unit, then at the site.
The first one found wins, so a VLAN on a token beats the one on the user or the site.
.br
To assign a VLAN you need the \fBassign\fR ACL on the VLAN object.
So you can give somebody the right to hand out one VLAN, and no other.
.br
A VLAN of another site is written as \fIsite\fB/\fIvlan\fR.
This is needed when all users live on the master site while each site has its own VLANs.
Whether such an assignment counts is decided by the site that answers the RADIUS request, through its \fBvlan_trusts\fR.
.br
Assign at most one VLAN per site.
The site that answers the RADIUS request returns its own VLAN.
If none of the VLANs belongs to it, it returns the first one its \fBvlan_trusts\fR allow, in the order of those entries.
Without a matching entry it returns no VLAN at all.
.br
What the switch gets as \fBTunnel\-Private\-Group\-Id\fR is the VLAN ID of the VLAN object, or its name if it has no VLAN ID.
The assignment is stored by UUID, so a VLAN can be renamed without losing it.
.br
Object types: site, unit, host, device, user, token
.TP
.B vlan_trusts \fR(list)
Which VLAN assignments this site accepts when it answers a RADIUS request.
The port belongs to the network of this site, whoever owns the VLAN object, so this site decides.
This parameter is the only place where that is decided.
Without a matching entry the assignment is ignored.
.br
An entry is needed as soon as the object or the VLAN belongs to another site.
Only an own VLAN for an own object works without one.
.br
An entry looks like \fIsite\fR[\fB:\fIvlan_site\fR[\fB/\fIvlan\fR]].
\fIsite\fR is the site of the object the VLAN is assigned to, \fIvlan_site\fB/\fIvlan\fR is the VLAN itself.
What you leave out means "all".
.br
So the entries on site \fIberlin\fR mean:
.RS
.TP
.B munich
allows all VLANs for objects of site munich
.TP
.B munich:berlin
allows all VLANs of site berlin for objects of site munich
.TP
.B munich:berlin/home
allows the VLAN \fIberlin/home\fR for objects of site munich
.TP
.B munich:munich/clients
allows the VLAN \fImunich/clients\fR for objects of site munich, which berlin then switches in its own network
.TP
.B berlin:munich/clients
allows the VLAN \fImunich/clients\fR for objects of site berlin itself
.RE
.IP
If an object has VLANs of several sites, the VLAN of this site wins; after that the order of the entries decides.
.br
The \fBassign\fR ACL on the VLAN object is no replacement for an entry: it is checked by the site that makes the assignment, against its own copy of the VLAN, so it says nothing about what this site allows.
See
.BR otpme\-vlan (1).
.br
Object types: site
.SS SSO Portal
.TP
.B device_token_roles \fR(list)
Comma separated list of roles the SSO portal adds device tokens to.
A value is a role of the current site, or \fIsite\fB/\fIrole\fR for a role of another site.
The value is looked up on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
The role's info text is shown in the portal settings, in the language of whoever is looking (\fBotpme\-role info \-\-language\fR).
The text written without a language is the administrator's own note and is never shown.
.br
One list serves every portal the user signs in at.
A portal only offers roles of its own site, so each one shows its own part of the list.
A role of another site is therefore not a mistake.
It is what the user gets at that site's portal, if that portal allows it via
.BR device_token_roles_trusts .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B device_token_roles_trusts \fR(list)
Which of this site's roles the SSO portal offers to users of other sites.
Naming a role in the user's
.B device_token_roles
is the home site's half of the decision, this parameter is the portal's half.
This parameter is the only place where that is decided.
Users of this site need no entry.
.br
An entry is \fIsite\fR or \fIsite\fB:\fIrole\fR.
\fIsite\fR is the home site of the user, \fIrole\fR is a role of \fIthis\fR site.
No other role is ever offered here.
Both kinds of entry are needed.
.br
So the entries on site \fImunich\fR mean:
.RS
.TP
.B berlin
allows device tokens here at all for users of site berlin
.TP
.B berlin:wlan\-users
allows this site's role \fIwlan\-users\fR for users of site berlin, if their own \fBdevice_token_roles\fR names it
.RE
.IP
Without the bare site entry a user of that site is offered nothing here.
A role without its own entry is left out, even when the user's
.B device_token_roles
names it.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B device_token_suffix \fR(str)
Makes a role usable for SSO device tokens.
A role without this parameter is not shown in the SSO portal at all, so it works as the opt\-in switch for
.BR device_token_roles .
.br
The value is appended to the token name when the portal registers a device token under this role, with a \fB\-\fR in between: the device \fBiphone\fR under a role whose suffix is \fBwlan\fR becomes the token \fBiphone\-wlan\fR.
So the suffix is written without a leading dash.
.br
It is what tells two tokens of the same device apart where a user is in more than one of the \fBdevice_token_roles\fR \(em \fBiphone\-wlan\fR and \fBiphone\-vpn\fR.
.br
Object types: role
.TP
.B device_token_types \fR(list)
Comma\-separated list of the token types the SSO portal creates for this role's device tokens: \fBpassword\fR and/or \fBtotp\fR.
With more than one the user chooses per token, and the first one is the default.
Unset means \fBpassword\fR.
.br
A \fBtotp\fR device token is created with its PIN disabled, because the device has to produce the OTP on its own.
Its secret is shown once when it is created, with a QR code for an authenticator app.
.br
Object types: role
.TP
.B max_device_tokens \fR(int)
How many device tokens one user may hold in this role, between 1 and 128.
Unset means no limit.
Once the limit is reached the SSO portal shows no form to add another one, and adding one is refused.
.br
Object types: role
.TP
.B device_token_mschap \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a \fBpassword\fR device token the SSO portal creates for this role gets MSCHAP enabled, e.g. for WLAN with PEAP/MSCHAPv2.
It is off unless the role says so, because MSCHAP keeps the NT hash of the password on the server.
It only affects tokens created from then on.
A \fBtotp\fR device token is always created without MSCHAP.
.br
Object types: role
.TP
.B sso_allow_session_mgmt \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a user may see and end their own sessions on the Settings page of the SSO portal.
It is off by default, because the listing shows where the account is logged in: client or host, address, token and times.
That is something an install should hand out deliberately.
.br
Only the sessions of the portal's own site are listed, because that is where a login of this portal lives.
Ending a session also ends its child sessions and the application (OIDC) sessions below it.
The session the Settings page itself runs in is shown, but it cannot be ended there.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_profile_attributes \fR(list, default: uid,givenName,sn,mail,telephoneNumber,mobile,title,o,ou,departmentNumber,l,createTimestamp,modifyTimestamp)
Comma\-separated list of the LDAP attributes the Profile page of the SSO portal shows, in this order.
The page is reached by clicking the user name in the navigation bar.
It shows the photo, and of these attributes the ones the user has.
.br
What is there for the machines rather than for the user is left out by default: \fBuidNumber\fR, \fBgidNumber\fR, \fBhomeDirectory\fR, \fBloginShell\fR, \fBentryUUID\fR, and \fBcn\fR and \fBdisplayName\fR, which are built from the others.
.br
The page is answered by the user's home site, so the value is looked up there: first on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_profile_edit \fR(list)
Comma\-separated list of the LDAP attributes a user may change on the Profile page of the SSO portal, e.g. \fBmail,telephoneNumber,mobile\fR.
\fBjpegPhoto\fR allows setting and removing the photo.
Unset (the default) means nothing can be changed there.
.br
An attribute listed here is shown on the page even when
.B sso_profile_attributes
leaves it out, after the ones listed there.
Attributes the user's extensions keep read\-only, like \fBuid\fR, cannot be changed even when they are listed.
.br
The change is made on the user's home site, so the value is looked up there: first on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
Whether a change needs a fresh authentication is
.BR sso_profile_edit_reauth .
.br
One change may carry up to 16 values of 1024 characters each.
A photo must be a JPEG of at most 8 MiB, and of at most 512 KiB once it has been resized to
.BR user_photo_dimensions .
A photo of another size is only stored after the user agreed to have it resized.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_passkeys \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether passkeys (FIDO2 resident credentials) are accepted as a login method on this site.
Set it to false to refuse passkey logins completely.
The login mask of the SSO portal stays the same.
.br
It resolves fail\-open: only an explicit false blocks.
.br
A user of another site only gets the Settings card when this site lists the user's home site under
.BR sso_allow_passkeys_trusts .
There is no fallback to this site's own setting.
The card also needs
.BR sso_allow_passkey_mgmt .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_passkey_mgmt \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a user may manage their own passkeys on the Settings page of the SSO portal: listing, registering, enabling and disabling, deleting.
It only works on top of
.BR sso_allow_passkeys ,
which still has to allow passkeys.
.br
It is off by default, because which credentials a user may add or remove on their own is something an install should hand out deliberately.
Signing in with a passkey is not affected.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_passkeys_trusts \fR(list)
Comma\-separated list of sites this site trusts with passkeys of users of other sites.
It has to be set on
.B both
sites.
Each of them makes a decision of its own, and each can only read its own list, because the parameter is not synced to other sites.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
On the site whose SSO portal the user stands in front of, it lists the home sites of the users.
This protects the portal site's backend and access groups from users of other sites.
A passkey registered here is written into this site's backend and added to this site's SSO access group, roles and groups.
So it gives a user of another site a new way in here.
Without an entry the Settings card is not offered: listing, registering, enabling and disabling passkeys.
.IP \(bu 2
On the user's home site, it lists the portal sites.
This protects the users of the home site from other sites.
The portal site runs the registration and decides which authenticator is bound to the account, so whoever controls that site could bind one of their own.
The home site only accepts such a request from a site in its list.
It tells the requesting site by the certificate of its node, not by anything the request says.
.RE
.IP
Neither side can make the other's decision.
The home site protects its users from other sites, the portal site protects its backend and access groups from users of other sites.
A site not in the list gets nothing.
There is no fallback to the local
.BR sso_allow_passkeys .
Users of this site need no entry.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_allow_fido2_deploy \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether FIDO2 may be chosen when the portal hands a user a new SSO token.
That happens on the deploy page of a first login, and for the new token of a token recovery.
Set it to false to leave FIDO2 to the administrator.
.br
It does not say whether a recovery runs at all.
That is decided by
.B allow_sso_account_recovery
and
.BR allow_sso_token_recovery ,
which look at the token the user already has.
.br
Registering further keys on the Settings page is decided by
.BR sso_allow_fido2 ,
which this parameter narrows.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B force_fido2_rp \fR(str, default: realm)
Which Relying Party ID a FIDO2 token is registered for.
\fBrealm\fR uses the realm name, \fBsso_fqdn\fR uses the SSO FQDN of the site.
It only applies to FIDO2 tokens; a passkey always uses the SSO FQDN of the site.
.br
The RP ID is written to the token when it is registered, and the login takes it from there.
So a change only applies to keys registered from then on, and keys already registered keep working.
.br
A browser only hands out a credential when the RP ID matches the page it is on: it has to be the FQDN of the page or a domain above it.
So \fBrealm\fR only works when the FQDN of the portal is below the realm name.
\fBsso_fqdn\fR always matches the portal of its own site.
.br
This does not make one token work at several portals.
Every site has its own SSO token, see
.BR default_sso_token_name .
.br
Valid values: \fBrealm\fR, \fBsso_fqdn\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B fido2_decoy_cred_id_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Length in bytes of the decoy credential IDs the FIDO2 login hands out when the user is unknown or has no security key.
The decoys are there so that the answer looks the same either way.
.br
All a browser gets to see is the credential ID and how many of them there are.
So a length that no authenticator in your realm produces gives the decoy away in a single request.
64 is what a YubiKey hands out.
Allowed values are 16 to 1023.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B fido2_decoy_max_creds \fR(int, default: 2)
How many decoy credentials are handed out at most.
The number is derived from the user name, so it is the same on every attempt, and it is spread over 1 to this value.
.br
A fixed count of one would give away every user who has a second security key, by the length of the list alone.
Set it to the largest number of security keys your users really carry.
1 switches the variation off.
The lowest value is 1; there is no upper limit.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_allow_totp_deploy \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether TOTP may be chosen when the portal hands a user a new SSO token.
That happens on the deploy page of a first login, and for the new token of a token recovery.
Set it to false to leave TOTP to the administrator.
.br
It does not say whether a recovery runs at all.
That is decided by
.B allow_sso_account_recovery
and
.BR allow_sso_token_recovery ,
which look at the token the user already has.
.br
Adding further authenticator apps on the Settings page is decided by
.BR sso_allow_totp ,
which this parameter narrows.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B sso_allow_totp \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether TOTP tokens may be used in the SSO portal at all.
That covers signing in with the PIN followed by the code of an authenticator app, and managing authenticator apps on the Settings page.
Managing them also needs
.BR sso_allow_totp_mgmt .
.br
Set it to false to refuse TOTP logins to the portal and to hide the card.
It takes
.B sso_allow_totp_deploy
with it.
Other access groups are not affected.
.br
It is checked after the token was verified, so a code sent with a refused token is spent all the same.
For a user of another site both the user's own value and this site have to allow it.
.br
It resolves fail\-open: only an explicit false blocks.
So adding the parameter underneath a running installation cannot switch off a login that works today.
.br
A user of another site gets the Settings card only when this site lists the user's home site under
.BR sso_allow_totp_trusts .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_totp_mgmt \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a user may manage their own authenticator apps on the Settings page of the SSO portal: listing, adding, enabling and disabling, deleting, making one the default token, and setting a new PIN on one.
It only works on top of
.BR sso_allow_totp ,
which still has to allow TOTP.
.br
It is off by default, because which credentials a user may add or remove on their own is something an install should hand out deliberately.
Signing in with a TOTP token is not affected.
.br
Making one the default token also needs the current default token to be one the portal manages: FIDO2, tiqr or TOTP, each with its own
.B sso_allow_*_mgmt
on.
A default token the administrator chose is never replaced or renamed from the Settings page.
.br
With it off the administrator manages the tokens of the user.
The Settings page then offers one thing instead: changing the PIN of the OTP token the user signed in with, and it asks for the current PIN.
With it on that form is gone, and PINs are set on the authenticator apps in the card.
.br
Setting a new PIN on an authenticator app asks for no current PIN.
The proof is the fresh authentication of
.BR deploy_totp_token_reauth ,
so a user who forgot the PIN can set a new one.
The secret stays, so the app keeps working.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_totp_trusts \fR(list)
Comma\-separated list of sites this site trusts with authenticator apps (TOTP tokens) of users of other sites.
It has to be set on
.B both
sites.
Each of them makes a decision of its own, and each can only read its own list, because the parameter is not synced to other sites.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
On the site whose SSO portal the user stands in front of, it lists the home sites of the users.
This protects the portal site's backend and access groups from users of other sites.
A token added here is written into this site's backend and added to this site's SSO access group, roles and groups.
So it gives a user of another site a new way in here.
Without an entry the Settings card is not offered: listing, adding, enabling and disabling authenticator apps.
.IP \(bu 2
On the user's home site, it lists the portal sites.
This protects the secrets of the home site's users from other sites.
The secret and the PIN of a new authenticator app are made on the home site, but they are shown by the portal site, so whoever controls that site can capture them and sign in as the user.
The home site only accepts such a request from a site in its list.
It tells the requesting site by the certificate of its node, not by anything the request says.
.RE
.IP
Neither side can make the other's decision.
The home site protects the secrets of its users, the portal site protects its backend and access groups from users of other sites.
A site not in the list gets nothing.
There is no fallback to the local
.BR sso_allow_totp .
Users of this site need no entry.
.br
Without the trust the Settings card is not offered at all, so an authenticator app cannot be deleted there either.
Signing in with one is not affected.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_allow_password_deploy \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a password may be chosen when the portal hands a user a new SSO token.
That happens on the deploy page of a first login, and for the new token of a token recovery.
.br
It is off by default, because a password is a weaker credential than TOTP or a security key.
An install has to decide for it.
.br
It does not say whether a recovery runs at all.
That is decided by
.B allow_sso_account_recovery
and
.BR allow_sso_token_recovery ,
which look at the token the user already has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B sso_allow_tiqr_deploy \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether tiqr may be chosen when the portal hands a user a new SSO token.
That happens on the deploy page of a first login, and for the new token of a token recovery.
.br
It is off by default, because tiqr needs a
.B tiqr_secret
on the site and the tiqr app on a phone.
So it should be a deliberate choice, not a button that appears on every install.
.br
It does not say whether a recovery runs at all.
That is decided by
.B allow_sso_account_recovery
and
.BR allow_sso_token_recovery ,
which look at the token the user already has.
.br
Enrolling further phones on the Settings page is decided by
.BR sso_allow_tiqr ,
which this parameter narrows.
A type nobody may sign in with is not one to hand somebody as their new token.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B sso_allow_tiqr \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether tiqr may be used in the SSO portal at all.
That covers signing in by confirming on a phone, and managing phones on the Settings page, enrolling one included.
Managing them also needs
.BR sso_allow_tiqr_mgmt .
.br
Set it to false to refuse tiqr logins completely and to hide the card.
.br
It resolves fail\-open: only an explicit false blocks.
So adding the parameter underneath a running installation cannot switch off a login that works today.
.br
This one defaults to
.IR true ,
while
.B sso_allow_tiqr_deploy
defaults to
.IR false .
That is what keeps tiqr out of the way on an installation that never set it up: a tiqr login needs a phone somebody enrolled deliberately.
.br
A user of another site gets the Settings card only when this site lists the user's home site under
.BR sso_allow_tiqr_trusts .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_tiqr_mgmt \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a user may manage their own tiqr phones on the Settings page of the SSO portal: listing, enrolling, enabling and disabling, deleting, making one the default token.
It only works on top of
.BR sso_allow_tiqr ,
which still has to allow tiqr.
.br
It is off by default, because which credentials a user may add or remove on their own is something an install should hand out deliberately.
Signing in with a phone is not affected.
.br
Making one the default token also needs the current default token to be one the portal manages, see
.BR sso_allow_totp_mgmt .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_tiqr_trusts \fR(list)
Comma\-separated list of sites this site trusts with tiqr phones of users of other sites.
It has to be set on
.B both
sites.
Each of them makes a decision of its own, and each can only read its own list, because the parameter is not synced to other sites.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
On the site whose SSO portal the user stands in front of, it lists the home sites of the users.
This protects the portal site's backend and access groups from users of other sites.
An enrolled phone is written into this site's backend and added to this site's SSO access group, roles and groups.
So it gives a user of another site a new way in here.
Without an entry the Settings card is not offered: listing, enrolling, enabling and disabling phones.
.IP \(bu 2
On the user's home site, it lists the portal sites.
This protects the secrets of the home site's users from other sites.
A phone delivers its tiqr secret to the site whose QR code it scanned, and that site passes it on to the home site.
So whoever controls that site can capture the secret and sign in as the user.
The home site only accepts such a request from a site in its list.
It tells the requesting site by the certificate of its node, not by anything the request says.
.RE
.IP
Neither side can make the other's decision.
The home site protects the secrets of its users, the portal site protects its backend and access groups from users of other sites.
A site not in the list gets nothing.
There is no fallback to the local
.BR sso_allow_tiqr .
Users of this site need no entry.
.br
Without the trust the Settings card is not offered at all, so a phone cannot be deleted there either.
Signing in with a phone is not affected: the phone answers a login with a one\-time response, and its secret stays on the phone.
.br
FIDO2 security keys need no such list, because a key never hands out its secret.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_allow_fido2 \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether FIDO2 security keys may be used in the SSO portal at all.
That covers signing in with one, and managing them on the Settings page, registering another included.
Managing them also needs
.BR sso_allow_fido2_mgmt .
.br
Set it to false to refuse FIDO2 logins completely and to hide the card.
It takes
.B sso_allow_fido2_deploy
with it.
.br
It resolves fail\-open: only an explicit false blocks.
FIDO2 login is older than this parameter and must not stop working because nobody set it.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_fido2_mgmt \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a user may manage their own security keys on the Settings page of the SSO portal: listing, registering, enabling and disabling, deleting, making one the default token.
It only works on top of
.BR sso_allow_fido2 ,
which still has to allow FIDO2.
.br
It is off by default, because which credentials a user may add or remove on their own is something an install should hand out deliberately.
Signing in with a key is not affected.
.br
Making one the default token also needs the current default token to be one the portal manages, see
.BR sso_allow_totp_mgmt .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_max_fido2_token \fR(int)
.TP
.B sso_max_passkey_token \fR(int)
.TP
.B sso_max_tiqr_token \fR(int)
.TP
.B sso_max_totp_token \fR(int)
How many security keys, passkeys, tiqr phones or authenticator apps one user may hold in the Settings card of an SSO portal, between 1 and 128.
Unset means no limit.
.br
Counted are the tokens the card lists: the ones added at this portal, and the default token when it is of that type.
Once the limit is reached the card shows no form to add another one, and adding one is refused.
That is checked on the user's home site, when the add starts and again when it completes.
.br
Tokens an administrator creates, a deploy of the default token and signing in are not affected.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_show_fido2_button \fR(bool, default: true)
.TP
.B sso_show_tiqr_button \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether the login mask offers the \fBSecurity Key\fR and \fBSign in with tiqr\fR buttons.
It only says what the page shows.
Whether the method may be used is
.B sso_allow_fido2
and
.BR sso_allow_tiqr .
.br
It is a site parameter, because the mask is drawn before anybody has typed a name.
A per\-user lookup would mean answering questions about who exists.
.br
It resolves fail\-open: only an explicit false hides a button.
.br
The button is the only way into that flow from the login mask, so hiding it does take the method away in practice.
.br
The re\-authentication page does not use this parameter.
There the user is known, and the page draws the one control their token can answer: a password field, the security key button or the tiqr button.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_show_recover_link \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether the login page shows the \fBCan't sign in?\fR link.
It is off by default.
.br
It works next to
.BR allow_sso_account_recovery ,
which is read per user.
This one hides the way in for the whole site, so a site that offers no recovery at all does not show the flow.
With it off, \fB/recover\fR redirects back to \fB/login\fR.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B tiqr_ocra_suite \fR(str, default: OCRA\-1:HOTP\-SHA1\-6:QH10\-S)
OCRA suite (RFC 6287) offered to the app when a phone is enrolled.
.br
The suite a token uses is stored on the token itself, because the apps bake it into the account at enrollment.
So a change only applies to phones enrolled from then on, and it never invalidates an existing one.
.br
The default is what the tiqr apps have always used.
Its bare
.B \-S
is a tiqr deviation from the RFC and means a 64 byte session field.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B tiqr_challenge_expiry \fR(int, default: 180)
Seconds a tiqr login challenge stays answerable.
The session key in the QR code carries its own creation time, so this bounds the window without any state on the server.
.br
There is no counter for wrong answers.
Counting them would mean writing state every node can see, from a route nobody has to authenticate to.
So what bounds guessing of the six digit response is this window together with
.BR sso_rate_limit_tiqr .
At the defaults that is 90 tries per challenge, each one worth one in a million.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B tiqr_enrollment_expiry \fR(int, default: 300)
Seconds an enrollment grant stays valid.
That is how long the user has to scan the enrollment QR code and finish in the app.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B tiqr_service_display_name \fR(str)
Name the tiqr app shows for this service in its account list.
Unset means the realm name is used.
.br
The app files the account under an identifier made from this name, and that identifier becomes the host part of the authentication URL.
So only characters that may appear there are accepted: letters, digits and
.BR "-._~!$&'()*+,;=" .
Setting a name with any other character is refused, and the message names the characters.
.br
A space is a good example of why.
Enrollment would still work, because the app gets that part as JSON.
But the login URL would be unparsable and the app would refuse every QR code from then on.
.br
The identifier is written in lower case, because the app parses the URL as one.
The display name keeps the case it was given.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B tiqr_auth_scheme \fR(str, default: tiqrauth)
URL scheme of the authentication link handed to the phone.
It has to match the app the users have, and the stock tiqr apps register this one.
Change it only when you ship your own build of the app.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B tiqr_enroll_scheme \fR(str, default: tiqrenroll)
URL scheme of the enrollment link handed to the phone, alongside
.BR tiqr_auth_scheme .
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_rate_limit_login \fR(str, default: 100/minute)
Rate limit per IP address on the \fB/login\fR POST route of the SSO portal.
It is a coarse guard against a flood from one source, and it is generous on purpose, so that a NAT pool does not trip it under normal load.
.br
The value is a Flask\-Limiter expression, e.g. \fB100/minute\fR or \fB1000/hour\fR.
Several limits can be chained with a semicolon, e.g. \fB3/minute; 1000/hour\fR.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_rate_limit_login_user \fR(str, default: 10/minute)
Rate limit per user name on the \fB/login\fR POST route of the SSO portal.
This one is about guessing one account, whatever address the tries come from.
It works next to the \fBauto_disable\fR policy of authd.
.br
The key is the submitted user name, so users behind one NAT address do not share a bucket.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_rate_limit_settings \fR(str, default: 60/minute)
Rate limit on the \fB/settings/*\fR routes of the SSO portal and on the routes that change a credential: registering a token, changing a password or a PIN, and so on.
The user has signed in on all of them.
.br
The key is the name of the signed in user, so many tabs or a runaway poll loop of one user cannot use up a shared bucket.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_rate_limit_recover \fR(str, default: 20/minute)
Rate limit on the recovery routes of the SSO portal (\fB/recover\fR, \fB/recover/complete/*\fR), which nobody has to authenticate to.
It bounds mail flooding and guessing of recovery links.
A real recovery is a handful of requests.
.br
The key is the submitted user name, so users behind one NAT address do not share a bucket.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_rate_limit_tiqr \fR(str, default: 30/minute)
Rate limit per IP address on the routes the tiqr apps call: \fB/tiqr/metadata\fR, \fB/tiqr/enroll\fR and \fB/tiqr/auth\fR.
Nobody has to authenticate to them.
.br
The auth route takes the six digit response of a phone, and there is no counter for wrong answers, so this limit and
.B tiqr_challenge_expiry
are what bound guessing.
Lower one of them if the default margin is not the one you want.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B auth_jwt_valid \fR(time, default: 1m)
How long a JWT for a cross\-site authentication stays valid.
.br
A user authenticates against one site and then uses another site of the same realm.
The site that did the authentication issues a short lived JWT, and the other site verifies the authentication with it.
.br
Keep the value short, so that a leaked JWT can only be replayed for a short time.
The value takes time units, e.g. \fB60s\fR or \fB2m\fR, see \fBCONFIG PARAMETERS\fR above.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B sso_jwt_valid \fR(time, default: 1D)
Validity of the JWT that the SSO portal
.RB ( httpd )
uses to authenticate against
.B ssod
for a signed in user, for example to register or delete a device token.
.br
The JWT is also the lifetime of the user's browser session in the portal.
When it expires, the user is signed out.
.br
Choose a value a little shorter than the session timeout of the SSO access group, so that the JWT decides when the user is signed out and not the access group session.
The value takes time units, e.g. \fB12h\fR or \fB1D\fR.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B add_device_token_to_trash \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether a device token the user deletes on the Settings page of the SSO portal goes to the trash instead of being deleted at once.
It can be restored from there with
.BR otpme\-trash (1).
Set it to false to delete such a token right away.
.br
The value is looked up on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B add_fido2_token_to_trash \fR(bool, default: true)
The same for a security key the user deletes on the Settings page.
.br
Restoring one puts the token back, not the credential on the key.
The key still holds the credential, so the restored token works again.
.br
There is one parameter per type, because what they hold differs.
A device token is a password somebody typed into a mail client, a security key is a credential that exists only on that one device.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B add_passkey_to_trash \fR(bool, default: true)
The same for passkeys, see
.BR add_fido2_token_to_trash .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B add_tiqr_token_to_trash \fR(bool, default: true)
The same for a phone the user removes on the Settings page.
Restoring one puts the token back with its secret, so the phone that was enrolled against it works again without being enrolled anew.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B add_totp_token_to_trash \fR(bool, default: true)
The same for an authenticator app the user removes on the Settings page.
Restoring one puts the token back with its secret and its PIN, so the app still produces valid codes for it.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_allow_login_token_redeploy \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether a user may deploy their login token again from the Settings page of the SSO portal.
That replaces it with a new token, of a type the
.B sso_allow_*_deploy
parameters allow.
.br
Turn it off where the administrator manages the login tokens of the users.
Otherwise a user could swap the token they were given for one of their own choosing.
The forced deploy of a token flagged for SSO deploy is not affected.
.br
The value is looked up on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B deploy_login_token_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether deploying the default token again through the SSO portal needs a fresh authentication.
What that flow hands out replaces the token the user signs in with, so without this an unlocked browser is enough to take over the account.
.br
The user is sent to the re\-auth page of the portal and lands back on the deploy page.
How long such an authentication stays fresh is
.BR sso_reauth_timeout .
.br
The recovery deploy is not covered by this.
It runs from the link in the recovery mail and has no session to prove anything with, so the link itself is the proof.
.br
Neither is the enrollment a token asks for itself, the one \fBotpme-token enable_sso_deploy\fR sets \(em whatever this parameter says.
There the token the user signed in with is the only credential they have, and it is a password token, so the re\-auth would ask for the same password a second time and say nothing.
It stays for every other flow, where a token of their own already exists: an OTP is good for one authentication, so whoever phished one gets a session and is then asked for a second OTP they do not have.
.br
The value is looked up on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B deploy_fido2_token_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
The same before a security key is registered on the Settings page.
Every one of these enrolls a second way into the account, and the new credential outlives the session it was created in.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B deploy_passkey_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
The same before a passkey is registered, see
.BR deploy_fido2_token_reauth .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B deploy_tiqr_token_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
The same before a phone is enrolled, see
.BR deploy_fido2_token_reauth .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B deploy_totp_token_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
The same before an authenticator app is added, see
.BR deploy_fido2_token_reauth .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B deploy_device_token_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
The same before a device token is added, see
.BR deploy_fido2_token_reauth .
.br
This is the one whose credential is a password on a screen, not a device the user has to be holding.
So it is also the one an install is most likely to find in the way.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_profile_edit_reauth \fR(bool, default: true)
The same before an attribute or the photo is changed on the Profile page, see
.BR sso_allow_profile_edit .
.br
That is no credential, but a mail address or a phone number can be where a password reset or a second factor is sent to.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B sso_reauth_timeout \fR(int, default: 120)
How long an authentication on the SSO portal stays fresh, in seconds.
The value takes time units, e.g. \fB2m\fR, and the lowest value is 60.
.br
It applies to every gate that asks for one: changing the recovery mail, the \fBdeploy_*_reauth\fR flows and
.BR sso_profile_edit_reauth .
It is short on purpose.
It is a window the user is working in, not a second session.
.br
The Settings page asks for a new authentication once less than 30 seconds of the window are left.
That applies to every add form \(em security key, passkey, phone, authenticator app and device token \(em and to the Profile editor.
The user still has to type a name and touch a key after the form opens, and a window that runs out in between would cost them a second trip.
.br
The value is looked up on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B reverse_proxy_ips \fR(list)
Comma separated list of IP addresses of trusted reverse proxies. When a request originates from one of these IPs, the
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
and
.B X\-Forwarded\-Host
headers are used to find the real client address and the requested host.
.br
A request from any other address ignores these headers, so a client cannot fake where it comes from by setting them itself.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B site_admin_blacklist \fR(list)
Comma separated list of object OIDs (for example
.BR user|myrealm/mysite/myuser )
that members of the
.B SITE_ADMIN
role must not reach through that role alone.
.br
Being in the role normally gives access to every object of the site, without any ACL check.
For a listed object that shortcut is dropped, and the ACLs on the object decide, like they do for anyone else.
So an ACL set on the object still gives access.
The list takes the blanket permission away, it does not override an ACL somebody granted on purpose.
.br
The list is set as OIDs but stored as UUIDs.
So an entry survives a rename of the object, and a new object that later takes over the name does not inherit it.
An OID of an object that does not exist is refused.
.br
The parameter is read from the site that handles the request, and it lives on the site object itself.
The role shortcut never applies there, so site admins cannot take entries out of their own blacklist.
That is the same protection
.BR realm ", " site " and " node
objects have anyway, without being listed.
.br
This applies to the object interfaces, i.e. management and API.
LDAP checks its ACLs on its own path and is not affected.
.br
Object types: site
.SS OIDC Provider
These parameters are set per OIDC client.
A value on the site or the unit is the default for the clients below it.
.br
An OIDC client is the application that sends the user to OTPme to sign in.
The OIDC specs call it the Relying Party, RP for short, and OTPme the OpenID Provider, OP.
.br
How the OP itself is run \(em switching it on, rotating the signing keys, the pairwise secret \(em is in
.BR otpme\-site (1).
.TP
.B oidc_default_scopes \fR(list)
Comma separated list of the scopes a new OIDC client is given.
It is read when the client is added, so a change does not touch the clients that are already there.
.br
A name has to be the name of an existing
.B scope
object, and setting an unknown one is refused.
A site starts with the scopes \fBopenid\fR, \fBprofile\fR, \fBemail\fR, \fBaddress\fR, \fBphone\fR, \fBoffline_access\fR and \fBgroups\fR, of which the first three are in this list.
.br
Example:
.RS
.PP
.B otpme\-site config \fIberlin\fB oidc_default_scopes openid,profile,email,groups
.RE
.IP
When the client is added,
.B \-\-scopes
replaces this list,
.B \-\-add\-scopes
comes on top of it, and
.B \-\-no\-default\-scopes
leaves it out, see
.BR otpme\-client (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B oidc_email_attribute \fR(str, default: mail)
The LDIF attribute the OIDC \fIemail\fR claim is taken from.
The default is \fBmail\fR, the attribute of inetOrgPerson.
.br
A site that keeps mail aliases in \fBmailLocalAddress\fR, the way postfix virtual or qmail do, sets that one here.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B oidc_logout_scope \fR(str, default: sso)
What the OIDC \fI/end_session\fR route does.
.RS
.TP
.B sso
signs the user out completely.
It also ends every OIDC session below the SSO session, and it sends a backchannel logout to each client that registered one.
.TP
.B rp
only ends the OIDC session of the client that called.
The SSO session and the other clients stay signed in.
.RE
.IP
\fBrp\fR is for a client that wants its own, shorter session, independent of the SSO session.
.br
Valid values: \fBsso\fR, \fBrp\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_pkce_required \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether PKCE (RFC 7636) is required in the authorize flow.
OAuth 2.1 requires it for every client, so it is on by default.
.br
Switch it off on a single client only, and only for an old one that cannot produce a \fBcode_verifier\fR and a \fBcode_challenge\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_allow_plain_pkce \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether the old PKCE method \fBplain\fR is accepted in the authorize flow.
.br
OAuth 2.1 §7.5.2 forbids \fBplain\fR, because whoever steals the authorization code has the verifier as well.
The only reason to turn it on is an old client that has \fBplain\fR built in and cannot be changed.
.br
The discovery document never offers \fBplain\fR, whatever this is set to.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_id_token_hint_max_age \fR(time, default: 3M, i.e. 90 days)
How old an \fBid_token_hint\fR may be that \fI/end_session\fR accepts.
.br
The \fBexp\fR of the token is not enforced on purpose.
A user who signs out an hour after the access token expired is a normal case.
But without any bound a years old ID Token from a backup could still drive a logout.
.br
Lower the value where you need more assurance.
It takes time units, e.g. \fB7D\fR, \fB12h\fR or \fB2W\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_access_token_ttl \fR(time, default: 1h)
How long an OIDC access token is valid, and with it the ID Token issued next to it.
The default of one hour is what OAuth 2.1 suggests for bearer tokens.
.br
Set it lower on a client that guards something valuable, e.g. \fB5m\fR, and higher on an internal tool, e.g. \fB8h\fR.
A refresh token is not bound by this value but by the SSO session above it.
.br
It takes time units, e.g. \fB5m\fR, \fB1h\fR or \fB8h\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_acr_scheme \fR(str, default: numeric)
Which Authentication Context Class Reference (\fIacr\fR) an ID Token carries.
.RS
.TP
.B numeric
writes a number, which is what most clients understand: \fB0\fR when no factor is known, \fB1\fR with any factor, and \fB2\fR when the login was hardware backed or used more than one factor.
The numbers follow OIDC Core §2 and ISO/IEC 29115.
.TP
.B none
writes no \fIacr\fR claim at all.
.RE
.IP
The \fIamr\fR claim (RFC 8176), which says how the user signed in, is written whenever the token is known, whatever this is set to.
.br
Valid values: \fBnumeric\fR, \fBnone\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_id_token_user_claims \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether the user claims of the granted scopes are put into the ID Token as well.
Those are \fBprofile\fR with name, picture and so on, plus \fBemail\fR, \fBphone\fR, \fBaddress\fR and \fBgroups\fR.
.br
It is off by default.
In the authorization code flow OIDC Core §5.4 hands them out at \fI/userinfo\fR only, and an ID Token is often passed on as proof that the user signed in.
.br
Turn it on for a client that never calls \fI/userinfo\fR and reads the ID Token alone.
The \fBuser_oidc\fR app of Nextcloud works that way and gets no name, mail address or photo without it.
.br
The claims of the ID Token itself \(em \fBsub\fR, \fBauth_time\fR, \fBamr\fR, \fBacr\fR \(em are never replaced.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_avatar_dimensions \fR(str, default: 300x300)
The size, as \fIwidth\fBx\fIheight\fR in pixels, in which a client gets the user's photo that the \fBpicture\fR claim points to.
.br
The URL of that claim carries the client:
.RS
.PP
.I /oidc/avatar/<client_uuid>/<user_uuid>.jpg
.RE
.IP
The photo is resized when it is fetched, the same way as for
.BR user_photo_dimensions .
.br
The default is the square Nextcloud wants.
Set it to the value of
.B user_photo_dimensions
for a client that should get the photo as it is stored.
.br
The URL is not authenticated: whoever puts another client into it gets the same photo in that client's size.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.TP
.B oidc_require_consent \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether OTPme asks the user at \fI/authorize\fR whether the client may have the data.
.br
It is off by default.
Inside a company the administrator has already decided per scope who may grant what, so one more click costs the user time and decides nothing.
.br
Turn it on for a client that is open to the public or serves several tenants, where the user should agree themselves.
.br
An agreement is remembered per user and client, the first time it is given.
A request that asks for more scopes than the remembered one asks again.
The OIDC request parameter \fBprompt=consent\fR always shows the screen again, whatever was remembered.
.br
Object types: site, unit, client
.SS Share Notifications
.TP
.B send_share_notifications \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether the site tells the hosts that are online about a change of the share permissions, so that they can act on it at once, e.g. mount or unmount a share when it is enabled or disabled.
.br
The value is looked up in two steps.
First on the token that makes the change, and only there, without walking its parents.
That lets a user switch off the notifications their own changes cause.
If the token has no value, the object that is changed decides: the share or the user, then its unit and the units above it, then the site.
.br
The options \fB\-\-share\-notify\fR and \fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR of
.BR otpme\-share ", " otpme\-group ", " otpme\-role ", " otpme\-token " and " otpme\-user (1)
decide it for one command.
.br
Object types: site, unit, share, user, token
.SS Daemon Tuning
A daemon reads these when it starts.
So a change takes effect once that daemon was restarted:
.RS
.PP
.B otpme\-controld restart \fR[\fIdaemon\fR]
.RE
.PP
Without a daemon name every daemon is restarted.
.TP
.B authd_workers \fR(int, default: 16)
Number of preforked
.B authd
worker processes.
They stay for as long as the daemon runs, and that is what keeps their caches warm.
So set this for a normal day, not for the peak; the peak is what
.B authd_max_workers
is for.
.br
Every listening socket gets its own pool, so a node that listens on a unix socket and on TCP runs two of them.
.br
The value is looked up on the node, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B authd_max_workers \fR(int, default: 64)
How far an
.B authd
worker pool may grow while every one of its workers is busy.
.br
A worker is busy for as long as its connection lasts, and a login lasts until the user has typed their password or OTP.
So a whole department arriving in the morning needs more workers than the rest of the day does.
.br
Workers above
.B authd_workers
are forked when the pool runs full, and they are stopped again once it has not been full for a minute.
So the extra memory is only spent during the rush, and the resident workers keep seeing enough requests to hold their caches.
.br
A connection that arrives while the pool is at its maximum is not refused.
It waits in the listen backlog (\fBsocket_backlog\fR) until a worker is free.
That the maximum was reached is logged, at most once a minute:
.RS
.PP
All 64 workers busy and pool at its maximum. New connections wait in the listen backlog
.RE
.IP
Set this to the same value as
.B authd_workers
to keep the pool at a fixed size.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B authd_worker_threads \fR(int, default: 0)
How many connections one
.B authd
worker serves at the same time, each in a thread.
\fB0\fR, the default, keeps a worker on the one connection it accepted.
.br
A worker only uses threads once the pool sits at
.B authd_max_workers
and every worker in it is busy.
Up to that point another worker is the better answer, because two connections in one worker share one GIL.
At that point the choice is no longer a slower login but no login: the connection waits in the backlog with nobody to pick it up.
What makes it work is that a login spends most of its time waiting for the user to type or to touch their token, and a thread waiting for input holds no GIL.
.br
What it buys is room.
How many connections may sit in a login at once goes from
.B authd_max_workers
to that times this value.
The room is cheap: one more waiting connection costs a thread stack instead of a process with caches of its own, so this can be set much higher than the number of workers ever could.
.br
It is no defence against somebody who occupies connections on purpose.
They pay one place per connection either way, so a higher ceiling only raises what it costs them.
What keeps a connection from being held is
.BR authd_idle_timeout .
.br
With a value above 0 every connection of that worker is served in a thread, not only the ones after the first.
It has to be that way: only a worker inside its accept loop can hand a connection to a thread, and a worker that served the first one itself would be out of that loop before the pool is even known to be full.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B authd_idle_timeout \fR(int, default: 120)
Seconds an
.B authd
connection may sit there without asking anything before it is closed. A
worker serves one connection at a time, so a client that connects and then
says nothing holds one for good -- \fBauthd_workers\fR of those and nobody
authenticates anywhere in the realm any more. Only the wait for the next
request is counted, never the work on one, so a token that takes its time (a
push token waiting for the user to confirm) is not affected. Set to \fB0\fR
to turn it off.
.br
A login does wait here: after the server has asked for a password or OTP,
the next request arrives only once the user has typed it, so this cannot be
shorter than a person at a prompt needs. The default matches the
.BR sshd (8)
\fBLoginGraceTime\fR of 120 seconds, and
.BR login (1)
gives 60 seconds (\fBLOGIN_TIMEOUT\fR in
.BR login.defs (5)),
so nothing coming through PAM holds a connection longer than that anyway.
.br
Treat this as a reaper for silent and dead connections, not as a defence
against a deliberate attacker: whatever a real login is allowed here is
available to anyone who sends one request and then stops talking.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B socket_backlog \fR(int, default: 1024)
Length of the accept queue the kernel keeps for the listening sockets of the OTPme daemons.
A connection the kernel has finished on its own waits in there until a worker calls accept().
.br
This is what carries a login storm while every worker is busy.
A client whose connection waits in the queue notices nothing but the delay.
One that does not fit in is refused: on a unix socket that is an \fBECONNREFUSED\fR right away, on TCP the SYN is dropped and the client tries again a few seconds later.
.br
The kernel caps the value at \fB/proc/sys/net/core/somaxconn\fR, so raise that as well if you set a higher one.
.br
On a host this matters for
.B backupd
and for the unix socket of
.BR hostd .
The
.B httpd
of an SSO host runs gunicorn, which brings its own backlog, and
.B confd
listens on nothing at all.
.br
The value is looked up on the node or host, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins, and without a value anywhere the built\-in 1024 is used.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.TP
.B ldapd_processes \fR(int, default: 4)
Number of worker processes
.B ldapd
answers LDAP requests with.
One process cannot use more than one core, so raise this on a node where ldapd runs out of CPU.
The workers share the listen port with SO_REUSEPORT.
.br
Every worker keeps its own LDIF caches.
So more workers cost memory, and each single one sees fewer requests to keep its caches warm with \(em see also
.BR ldap_shared_cache .
.br
The value is looked up on the node, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B ldap_cache \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether the
.B ldapd
worker processes cache anything at all: the entries they built, the searches they answered, and the objects those came from.
.br
With it off every search builds its entries from the backend again.
That costs time on a search that is repeated, because encoding a result entry is more work than everything else a cached search does put together.
.br
What it buys is that nothing of a search outlives it.
A worker that answered one large search otherwise keeps those entries for as long as they stay valid, so its memory follows the largest search it has seen, once per worker.
Turn it off where that matters more than the time a search takes, or to find out whether a wrong answer comes from a cache.
.br
This is only about what a worker keeps for itself.
What the workers hand each other is a setting of its own and keeps working with this one off, so turn off
.B ldap_shared_cache
as well to have a worker answer every search from the backend.
.br
The value is looked up on the node, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B ldap_shared_cache \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether the
.B ldapd
worker processes share what they looked up with each other: search results and resolved search bases.
.br
With it off every worker has its own caches only.
That is useful as a comparison when you suspect the shared caches.
It only means something when
.B ldapd_processes
is greater than 1.
.br
It works next to
.BR ldap_cache .
What the workers share with each other is still shared when the per\-worker caches are off.
.br
The value is looked up on the node, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B ldap_shared_cache_time \fR(int, default: 3600)
Seconds a cached search result and a resolved search base stay valid in the
.B ldapd
caches.
It is also the expiry of the cache keys themselves, so nothing has to be cleaned up after a worker that went away.
.br
This is not what keeps the caches correct: a changed object drops its entries at once.
The value only says how long an entry nobody touched sits around, so lowering it costs hits and buys no correctness.
It applies to the shared caches as well as to the per\-worker ones.
.br
The value is looked up on the node, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B httpd_ssl_socket_uri \fR(str, default: tcp://[::]:443)
The socket the
.B httpd
of the SSO portal listens on, as \fBtcp://address:port\fR.
.br
The value is looked up on the host or node, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
So one SSO host can bind another port, for example when a reverse proxy sits in front of it.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.TP
.B httpd_socket_uri \fR(str, default: tcp://[::]:80)
The socket of the plain HTTP listener that serves the CA certificates of the realm and the site (\fBrealm_ca.pem\fR, \fBsite_ca.pem\fR), as \fBtcp://address:port\fR.
A host that has no OTPme trust yet gets them from there.
.br
The value is looked up like
.BR httpd_ssl_socket_uri .
To switch the listener off, set
.B httpd_workers
to 0.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.TP
.B httpd_ssl_workers \fR(int, default: 8)
Number of preforked
.B httpd
worker processes serving the SSO portal over HTTPS. Increase on busy SSO hosts to handle more concurrent TLS requests. The most specific match wins (node/host override unit overrides site).
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.TP
.B httpd_workers \fR(int, default: 2)
Number of preforked worker processes for the plain\-HTTP CA\-publish listener bound on
.BR httpd_socket_uri .
Set to 0 to disable the listener entirely (e.g. when port 80 is owned by a reverse proxy or ACME http\-01 responder).
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.TP
.B allow_who_from_hosts \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether \fBotpme\-tool who\fR may be run on a host that is not a node.
The request goes to
.BR idled ,
which answers who is logged in where.
.br
Off by default, so that only a node sees that list.
Set it to true to let the hosts of the site ask as well.
Turn it on where it is needed, not everywhere.
.br
Whatever it is set to, a peer of another site or of another realm is always refused.
.br
Object types: site
.TP
.B allow_user_messages \fR(list, default: empty)
The hosts that may send messages to the users of this site.
See
.B message
in
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.RS
.PP
Nodes may send messages whatever this is set to.
A host may not, until it is listed here.
Add a host where something on it has to reach the people who use it.
.PP
Give the host by name, several of them separated by commas, and
.B \-a
to add one to those already there:
.PP
.nf
otpme\-site config berlin allow_user_messages munich\-host1
otpme\-site config \-a berlin allow_user_messages munich\-host2
.fi
.PP
The hosts are kept by UUID and shown by name again.
A name belongs to a host only for as long as it has it, so a host that is removed takes its permission with it, and a new host of the same name does not inherit it.
.PP
A message is shown in a window on the desktop of the user, so a host that may send one can put text in front of somebody.
That is why it is one host at a time and not a switch for all of them, and why only an admin may change it.
.PP
Whatever it is set to, a peer of another site or of another realm is always refused.
.PP
Object types: site
.RE
.TP
.B preferred_master_node \fR(str)
Name of the node that
.B clusterd
should make the master of this site, as soon as that node is up and the cluster has quorum.
.br
If another node is master, clusterd hands the role over in an orderly way.
It waits until the cluster journal is worked off before it does.
If the preferred node is master already, nothing happens.
.br
The handover is done once after clusterd starts, not again while it runs.
A restart of the daemon makes it check again.
.br
Leave it unset to let the normal election pick any node that can take the role.
.br
Object types: site
.SS Connection Limits
How many connections a daemon serves at the same time.
Use them to keep a busy node from running out of connections, or to hold a quiet site back on purpose.
The defaults fit a normal installation; raise one only when the traffic asks for it.
.PP
A connection that arrives above the limit is closed at once.
It does not wait, which is the difference to
.BR socket_backlog ,
and the client sees the connection go away.
The daemon logs that it reached its limit, at most every five seconds, and says afterwards how many messages it left out.
.PP
Only the daemons below have such a parameter.
.B authd
has none: its limit is 1024, built in, and its worker pool does not enforce it \(em what bounds authd is
.B authd_max_workers
together with
.BR authd_worker_threads .
.B httpd
runs gunicorn with its own settings, and
.B hostd
and
.B confd
have no parameter either.
.PP
Each value is looked up on the node (or the host, where that is allowed), then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins.
.TP
.B mgmtd_max_conn \fR(int, default: 128)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR mgmtd .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B joind_max_conn \fR(int, default: 128)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR joind .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B syncd_max_conn \fR(int, default: 1024)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR syncd .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B ssod_max_conn \fR(int, default: 1024)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR ssod .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B clusterd_max_conn \fR(int, default: 512)
Maximum concurrent peer connections accepted by
.BR clusterd .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B fsd_max_conn \fR(int, default: 1024)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR fsd .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B idled_max_conn \fR(int, default: 1024)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR idled .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node
.TP
.B backupd_max_conn \fR(int, default: 256)
Maximum concurrent client connections accepted by
.BR backupd .
.br
It can also be set on a host, because a host can be the backup target that takes the backups.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.SS Request Limits
.TP
.B max_decompressed_size \fR(size, default: 256M)
How large a single request may be \fIafter\fR it was decompressed.
It guards against a small compressed message that blows up into something that eats the memory of the daemon.
.br
A daemon reads the value when it starts, from its own node or host object, and the decompress path enforces it.
.br
The value is looked up on the node or host, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins, and without a value anywhere the built\-in 256M is used.
.br
It takes human sizes, e.g. \fB256M\fR or \fB1G\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, host
.SS Description and Info Length Limits
.TP
.B max_description_len \fR(int, default: 1024)
How long the description of an object may be, in characters.
It is checked when the description is changed, never when an object is loaded, so a lower limit does not break what is already there.
.br
The value is looked up on the object, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins, and without a value anywhere the built\-in default is used.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.TP
.B max_info_len \fR(int, default: 16384)
How long the info text of an object may be, in characters, per language.
It is checked when the text is changed, never when an object is loaded.
.br
An info text is written per language (\fBotpme\-role info \-\-language\fR), and a few paragraphs fit in one.
Two things read it today:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The info of a role named by
.B device_token_roles
is shown in the Settings page of the SSO portal, so that the user reads what the role is for.
.IP \(bu 2
The info of the SSO access group of the site is the text of the recovery mail, one per language.
Without one OTPme sends the text it ships with, in the language of the user.
.RE
.IP
The value is looked up on the object, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins, and without a value anywhere the built\-in default is used.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.SS Name Length Limits
How long the name of an object may be, one parameter per object type.
.PP
It is checked when an object is added and when it is renamed, never when one is loaded.
So a lower limit leaves the objects that are already there alone, and they keep working.
.PP
Lower them to keep LDAP DNs, file paths and the output of the commands readable.
Raise one only when you know that what reads those names can take it.
.PP
Each value is looked up on the object, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins, and without a value anywhere the built\-in default is used.
.PP
All of them are of type \fBint\fR and can be set on
.BR site " and " unit .
.TP
.B max_realm_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for realm names.
.TP
.B max_site_name_len \fR(int, default: 32)
Cap for site names.
.TP
.B max_unit_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for unit names.
.TP
.B max_user_name_len \fR(int, default: 32)
Cap for user names.
.TP
.B max_token_name_len \fR(int, default: 128)
Cap for token names.
.TP
.B max_group_name_len \fR(int, default: 32)
Cap for group names.
.TP
.B max_role_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for role names.
.TP
.B max_accessgroup_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for access group names.
.TP
.B max_host_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for host names.
.TP
.B max_node_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for node names.
.TP
.B max_client_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for client names.
.TP
.B max_ca_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for CA names.
.TP
.B max_policy_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for policy names.
.TP
.B max_resolver_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for resolver names.
.TP
.B max_scope_name_len \fR(int, default: 128)
Cap for scope names.
.TP
.B max_script_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for script names.
.TP
.B max_share_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for share names.
.TP
.B max_pool_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for pool names.
.TP
.B max_dictionary_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for dictionary names.
.TP
.B max_device_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for device names.
.TP
.B max_vlan_name_len \fR(int, default: 64)
Cap for VLAN names.
.SS Backup
.TP
.B backup_enabled \fR(bool)
Whether this object is backed up at all.
Without it a backup of the object is refused, so it is the switch an administrator turns on once the other parameters are in place.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_exclude_special \fR(bool)
Whether special files are left out of the backup: block and character devices, FIFOs and sockets.
Only the file itself is meant, never the data behind a device.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_server \fR(str)
The node or host that takes the backups of this object.
.br
It is set by name, and it is shown as the FQDN of that node or host.
A node or host that does not exist is refused.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_time \fR(str)
The time of day a backup may run, as \fIHH:MM\fB\-\fIHH:MM\fR, e.g. \fB02:00\-03:00\fR.
Both times are checked when the value is set.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_interval \fR(time)
How much time passes between two backups of this object.
It takes time units, e.g. \fB1h\fR or \fB1D\fR, and it is shown in those units.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_retry_interval \fR(time, default: 5m)
How long a backup waits before it tries again, when the repository is in somebody else's hands.
That happens when another backup, a restore or a repack is running in it.
Such a backup has not failed, so it does not report one: the same attempt works once the other one is done.
It takes time units, e.g. \fB5m\fR, and it is shown in those units.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_retry_count \fR(int, default: 12)
How many more times a backup tries when it found the repository in use.
The tries end with the backup window in any case, so this only bounds a window that stands open all day.
With \fB0\fR a backup does not wait at all, and a repository in use makes it fail.
.br
The time of the last backup stays as it is while a backup waits.
No backup ran, so \fBbackup_interval\fR must not count the waiting as the backup of this window.
It is written when the backup runs, and when the last try gives up.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_key \fR(str)
The AES key the backups of this object are encrypted with, as 64 hex characters.
OTPme makes one from 32 random bytes when the object is created, so there is no need to set one by hand.
.br
It is stored encrypted with the key of the site.
\fBshow_config\fR shows it in clear to whoever may read it, and \fB<hidden>\fR where the site key is not available.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_repo_password \fR(str)
The password the backup server asks for.
.br
It is stored encrypted with the key of the site, like
.BR backup_key .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_report_enabled \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether a mail is sent about a backup run that finished, whether it worked or failed.
With it off none of the other
.B backup_report_*
parameters does anything.
.br
Where the mail is sent is the shared
.B smtp_relay_*
parameters below.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_report_mode \fR(str, default: all)
Which backup runs are reported, and how.
.RS
.TP
.B all
one mail per run
.TP
.B success
only a run that worked
.TP
.B error
only a run that failed
.TP
.B summary
one mail for all backup runs of the host, instead of one per backup object
.RE
.IP
The summary has one line per object: name, status, when it finished, how long it took, how many files, and how much was backed up and stored \(em or the error of a run that failed.
.br
It is an HTML mail with a table, failed runs in red, and it carries a plain text version for mail programs that show no HTML.
It is sent as soon as the last running backup has finished.
Objects with a different
.B backup_report_mail_to
get a summary of their own.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_report_mail_from \fR(str)
The address the report mail comes from, in the envelope and in \fBFrom:\fR.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_report_mail_to \fR(str)
The address the report mail goes to.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B smtp_relay_server \fR(str, default: 127.0.0.1)
The SMTP relay OTPme hands its mails to.
Every mail goes through it: the backup reports, the recovery mail of the SSO portal, and whatever comes later.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B smtp_relay_port \fR(int, default: 25)
TCP port of the SMTP relay.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B smtp_relay_starttls \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether OTPme sends \fBSTARTTLS\fR before it hands the message over.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B smtp_relay_auth \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether OTPme authenticates to the relay, with
.B smtp_relay_username
and
.BR smtp_relay_password .
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B smtp_relay_username \fR(str)
Login name for SMTP authentication.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B smtp_relay_password \fR(str)
The password for the SMTP authentication.
.br
It is stored encrypted with the key of the site, like
.BR backup_key .
\fBshow_config\fR shows \fB<hidden>\fR where that key is not available.
.br
Object types: site, unit, node, share
.TP
.B backup_mode \fR(str)
How the backup data is stored.
.RS
.TP
.B pack
in pack files, which needs less space
.TP
.B tree
in pack files and, on top of that, in a directory tree that looks like the files it came from
.RE
.IP
\fBtree\fR is what a restore share needs, because that share is mounted over FUSE and reads the tree.
.br
There is no default for every object.
Without this parameter a share is backed up as \fBtree\fR and a node as \fBpack\fR.
Setting it overrides that.
.br
Object types: node, share
.TP
.B backup_excludes \fR(list)
Comma separated list of patterns that are left out of the backup.
.br
Object types: node, share
.TP
.B backup_includes \fR(list)
Comma separated list of patterns that are taken into the backup.
.br
Object types: node, share
.TP
.B backup_script \fR(str)
The OTPme script
.B backupd
runs around every backup, as a path relative to the scripts unit.
.br
It is called with a
.B pre
hook before the backup and a
.B post
hook after it.
An exit code other than 0 stops the run.
.br
The default is the
.B backup_script.sh
that OTPme ships, in the default scripts unit.
Only an admin may set this parameter.
.br
Object types: site, unit, share, node
.SS HOTP Tokens
A value of this section is written to the token when the token is added, except where it says otherwise.
A later change of the parameter leaves the tokens that exist alone.
.TP
.B hotp_check_range \fR(int, default: 32)
How many counter values ahead of the one it has stored OTPme tries when a HOTP token signs in.
A token whose counter ran away further than that needs a resync.
.br
The value is written to the token as its counter check range.
Change it on a single token with \fBcounter_check_range\fR, see
.BR otpme\-token (1).
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B hotp_resync_check_range \fR(int, default: 1024)
How far a resync of a HOTP token looks, around the counter the token has stored.
It looks in both directions, so the window is twice this value.
.br
This one is read at every resync, not written to the token.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B hotp_default_pin_len \fR(int, default: 4)
How many digits the PIN of a new HOTP token has.
It is also the length of a PIN that \fBgen_pin\fR makes later.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B hotp_secret_len \fR(int, default: 10)
How many bytes the secret of a new HOTP token has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.SS TOTP Tokens
.TP
.B totp_default_pin_len \fR(int, default: 4)
How many digits the PIN of a new TOTP token has.
The SSO portal uses it as well when it adds an authenticator app.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B totp_secret_len \fR(int, default: 10)
How many bytes the secret of a new TOTP token has.
It is written to the token when the token is added.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.SS mOTP Tokens
The first three of these are written to the token when it is added, and they are used by a
.B yubikey_hmac
token as well, although their names say mOTP.
A later change leaves the tokens that exist alone.
.TP
.B motp_validity_time \fR(int, default: 60)
How many seconds an OTP of such a token is good for.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B motp_timedrift_tolerance \fR(int, default: 15)
How many seconds the clock of the token may be off.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B motp_len \fR(int, default: 6)
How many characters an OTP of such a token has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B motp_default_pin_len \fR(int, default: 4)
How many digits the PIN of a new mOTP token has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B motp_secret_len \fR(int, default: 16)
How many bytes the secret of a new mOTP token has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.SS Static Password Tokens
.TP
.B default_static_pass_len \fR(int, default: 10)
How many characters a generated password of a static password token has.
Which characters it may use comes from the password policy of the user.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.SS Hardware Tokens
.TP
.B check_fido2_attestation_cert \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether the attestation certificate of a FIDO2 token is verified when the token is deployed.
.br
Verified are two things: that the certificate is inside its validity dates, and that its signature comes from the FIDO2 CA certificate of the site that matches its issuer.
Those CA certificates live on the site object, see
.BR otpme\-site (1).
.br
It is off by default, because a deployment fails when the site has no CA certificate for the issuer of that key.
Turn it on where you hand out keys of a known make and have their CA certificate.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B check_u2f_attestation_cert \fR(bool, default: false)
The same for a U2F token.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS OTP Push Tokens
.TP
.B otp_push_default_pass_len \fR(int, default: 6)
How many characters a password is that OTPme generates and pushes to the user.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B default_otp_push_script \fR(str)
The script that delivers the password to the user, e.g. by SMS or by mail.
It is set as a path relative to the scripts unit, and the default is the \fBpush_script.sh\fR that OTPme ships.
.br
Object types: site, unit
.SS YubiKey HMAC Tokens
.TP
.B otpme_hmac_otp_len \fR(int, default: 16)
How many characters an OTP of such a token has.
It is written to the token when the token is added.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.TP
.B otpme_hmac_secret_len \fR(int, default: 16)
How many bytes the secret of such a token has.
.br
These tokens also use
.BR motp_validity_time " and " motp_timedrift_tolerance .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user
.SS Hash Parameters
These say how a password is hashed.
Only the parameters of the hash that
.B default_pw_hash_type
names are used; the others sit there without doing anything.
.PP
A value is looked up on the token, then on the user, then on the user's unit and the units above it, then on the site.
The first one found wins, and without a value anywhere the built\-in default is used.
.PP
A password hash keeps the values it was made with, so a change only applies to passwords set from then on.
.PP
Note the names: Argon2d spells it \fBiter\fR where Argon2i and Argon2id spell it \fBiterations\fR.
.SS Hash Parameters (Argon2i)
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2i_iterations \fR(int, default: 2)
How many passes Argon2i makes over its memory.
More passes cost more time.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2i_min_mem \fR(int, default: 65536)
The lower bound of the memory in KB, and
.B default_pw_hash_argon2i_max_mem
the upper one.
.br
Both are only used where the caller asks for the memory to be chosen at run time, and password hashing never does: it always uses 64 MiB.
The one place that asks is the key of the offline tokens, and that one takes its values from the options of
.BR pam_otpme (8),
not from here.
So changing these two changes nothing today.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2i_max_mem \fR(int, default: 262144)
See
.BR default_pw_hash_argon2i_min_mem .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2i_threads \fR(int, default: 1)
How many threads Argon2i works with.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2i_key_len \fR(int, default: 128)
How many bytes the hash has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS Hash Parameters (Argon2d)
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2d_iter \fR(int, default: 2)
How many passes Argon2d makes over its memory.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2d_min_mem \fR(int, default: 65536)
The lower bound of the memory in KB, see
.BR default_pw_hash_argon2i_min_mem .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2d_max_mem \fR(int, default: 262144)
The upper bound of the memory in KB, see
.BR default_pw_hash_argon2i_min_mem .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2d_threads \fR(int, default: 1)
How many threads Argon2d works with.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2d_key_len \fR(int, default: 128)
How many bytes the hash has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS Hash Parameters (Argon2id)
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2id_iterations \fR(int, default: 2)
How many passes Argon2id makes over its memory.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2id_min_mem \fR(int, default: 65536)
The lower bound of the memory in KB, see
.BR default_pw_hash_argon2i_min_mem .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2id_max_mem \fR(int, default: 262144)
The upper bound of the memory in KB, see
.BR default_pw_hash_argon2i_min_mem .
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2id_threads \fR(int, default: 1)
How many threads Argon2id works with.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_argon2id_key_len \fR(int, default: 128)
How many bytes the hash has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS Hash Parameters (PBKDF2)
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_pbkdf2_iter \fR(int, default: 100000)
How many rounds PBKDF2 makes.
More rounds cost more time.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_pbkdf2_algo \fR(str, default: SHA256)
The hash PBKDF2 works with.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_pbkdf2_key_len \fR(int, default: 128)
How many bytes the hash has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SS Hash Parameters (HKDF)
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_hkdf_algo \fR(str, default: SHA256)
The hash HKDF works with.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.TP
.B default_pw_hash_hkdf_key_len \fR(int, default: 32)
How many bytes the key has.
.br
Object types: site, unit, user, token
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/otpme/otpme.conf
The configuration of this host or node: which realm and site it belongs to, where its data lies, and how it talks to its backends.
The other files below
.I /etc/otpme/
are in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
.I /var/lib/otpme/
The data of this host: the objects, the indexes and the caches.
.TP
.I /var/log/otpme/
The log files, one per daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.otpme/otpme.conf
The settings of one user, e.g. which agent to talk to.
